-{"source": "safety_disclaimer", "section": "What This Tool Is", "text": "BreastCare AI is a research and educational prototype that estimates malignancy probability from four structured mammographic features: patient age, mass shape, mass margin, and mass density. It uses a LightGBM model trained on the UCI Mammographic Masses dataset (Elter, 2007) and produces SHAP-based feature attribution to explain its estimates.", "chunk_id": "safety_disclaimer_0"}
-{"source": "safety_disclaimer", "section": "What This Tool Is Not", "text": "This tool is not a medical device. It has not been cleared or approved by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) or any other regulatory authority. It is not intended for clinical diagnosis, treatment planning, or as a substitute for radiological interpretation.", "chunk_id": "safety_disclaimer_1"}
-{"source": "safety_disclaimer", "section": "What This Tool Is Not", "text": "This tool does not process mammogram images. It uses structured numeric features only. Its accuracy is limited by the small size of its training data (860 cases) and the sparsity of its feature set (4 features). Production clinical AI systems use 20 or more descriptors, calcification patterns, and full mammographic image analysis.", "chunk_id": "safety_disclaimer_2"}
-{"source": "safety_disclaimer", "section": "Clinical Limitations", "text": "The model was trained on data from a single institution and may not generalise to other screening programmes, imaging equipment, or patient populations. It has not been validated in a prospective clinical trial. Its performance in real-world screening settings is unknown.", "chunk_id": "safety_disclaimer_3"}
-{"source": "safety_disclaimer", "section": "Clinical Limitations", "text": "The model returns a point estimate without calibrated confidence intervals. The fixed decision threshold of 0.50 is not optimised for any clinical cost-benefit ratio.", "chunk_id": "safety_disclaimer_4"}
-{"source": "safety_disclaimer", "section": "High-Risk Estimate Notice", "text": "When the estimated malignancy probability is 65% or higher, this tool displays an enhanced notice. A high probability estimate does not mean a diagnosis of cancer. It means the features you entered are statistically associated with a higher likelihood of malignancy in the training data. Only biopsy and pathological examination can confirm or exclude malignancy.", "chunk_id": "safety_disclaimer_5"}
-{"source": "safety_disclaimer", "section": "What You Should Do", "text": "If you have received an abnormal mammography result, or if you have concerns about a breast finding, you should consult a qualified radiologist or physician. Do not use this tool's output to make or delay clinical decisions.", "chunk_id": "safety_disclaimer_6"}
-{"source": "safety_disclaimer", "section": "What You Should Do", "text": "All estimates produced by this tool must be reviewed by a qualified clinician before any clinical action is taken.", "chunk_id": "safety_disclaimer_7"}
-{"source": "safety_disclaimer", "section": "Privacy", "text": "This tool does not collect, store, or transmit personal health information. The feature values entered are used solely to compute and display a risk estimate within your session. No data is retained after the session ends.", "chunk_id": "safety_disclaimer_8"}
-{"source": "safety_disclaimer", "section": "Contact and Research Context", "text": "BreastCare AI was developed as an academic research prototype demonstrating the integration of gradient boosted tree models, SHAP explainability, FAISS retrieval-augmented generation, and Google Gemini for educational purposes in the context of breast cancer screening research.", "chunk_id": "safety_disclaimer_9"}
+{"chunk_id": "acr_followup_guidelines::bi-rads-assessment-categories-3-4-and-5-follow-up-and-biopsy::0", "source": "acr_followup_guidelines", "title": "BI-RADS Assessment Categories 3, 4, and 5 — Follow-Up and Biopsy", "section": "BI-RADS Assessment Categories 3, 4, and 5 — Follow-Up and Biopsy", "text": "The final BI-RADS assessment category sets how a finding is managed. The likelihoods of malignancy below are the ranges the American College of Radiology attaches to each category.", "index_text": "BI-RADS Assessment Categories 3, 4, and 5 — Follow-Up and Biopsy: The final BI-RADS assessment category sets how a finding is managed. The likelihoods of malignancy below are the ranges the American College of Radiology attaches to each category."}
+{"chunk_id": "acr_followup_guidelines::bi-rads-category-3-probably-benign::0", "source": "acr_followup_guidelines", "title": "BI-RADS Assessment Categories 3, 4, and 5 — Follow-Up and Biopsy", "section": "BI-RADS Category 3: Probably Benign", "text": "Category 3 means a likelihood of malignancy of 2% or less. It is used only after a complete diagnostic work-up, never at screening. Typical examples are a solitary non-calcified circumscribed solid mass, a focal asymmetry with no correlate on ultrasound, or a solitary group of round calcifications.\n\nCategory 3 findings are managed by surveillance rather than biopsy: usually a follow-up mammogram at 6 months, then bilateral mammograms at about 12 and 24 months. After about 2 to 3 years of stability, the finding is reassessed as benign (category 2), or earlier if it resolves. A biopsy may still be chosen after shared decision-making with the patient, for a valid clinical reason, or when follow-up is impractical. If the finding grows or changes suspiciously, it is upgraded and biopsied.", "index_text": "BI-RADS Assessment Categories 3, 4, and 5 — Follow-Up and Biopsy > BI-RADS Category 3: Probably Benign: Category 3 means a likelihood of malignancy of 2% or less. It is used only after a complete diagnostic work-up, never at screening. Typical examples are a solitary non-calcified circumscribed solid mass, a focal asymmetry with no correlate on ultrasound, or a solitary group of round calcifications.\n\nCategory 3 findings are managed by surveillance rather than biopsy: usually a follow-up mammogram at 6 months, then bilateral mammograms at about 12 and 24 months. After about 2 to 3 years of stability, the finding is reassessed as benign (category 2), or earlier if it resolves. A biopsy may still be chosen after shared decision-making with the patient, for a valid clinical reason, or when follow-up is impractical. If the finding grows or changes suspiciously, it is upgraded and biopsied."}
+{"chunk_id": "acr_followup_guidelines::bi-rads-category-4-suspicious::0", "source": "acr_followup_guidelines", "title": "BI-RADS Assessment Categories 3, 4, and 5 — Follow-Up and Biopsy", "section": "BI-RADS Category 4: Suspicious", "text": "Category 4 covers a likelihood of malignancy above 2% and below 95%. The recommended management is a tissue diagnosis, usually an image-guided core needle biopsy. The category is subdivided by level of suspicion.", "index_text": "BI-RADS Assessment Categories 3, 4, and 5 — Follow-Up and Biopsy > BI-RADS Category 4: Suspicious: Category 4 covers a likelihood of malignancy above 2% and below 95%. The recommended management is a tissue diagnosis, usually an image-guided core needle biopsy. The category is subdivided by level of suspicion."}
+{"chunk_id": "acr_followup_guidelines::bi-rads-category-4-suspicious-category-4a::0", "source": "acr_followup_guidelines", "title": "BI-RADS Assessment Categories 3, 4, and 5 — Follow-Up and Biopsy", "section": "BI-RADS Category 4: Suspicious > Category 4A", "text": "Category 4A means low suspicion for malignancy: above 2% and up to 10%. A biopsy is recommended, but a benign result is expected. After a benign result that is concordant with the imaging, the radiologist sets the follow-up.", "index_text": "BI-RADS Assessment Categories 3, 4, and 5 — Follow-Up and Biopsy > BI-RADS Category 4: Suspicious > Category 4A: Category 4A means low suspicion for malignancy: above 2% and up to 10%. A biopsy is recommended, but a benign result is expected. After a benign result that is concordant with the imaging, the radiologist sets the follow-up."}
+{"chunk_id": "acr_followup_guidelines::bi-rads-category-4-suspicious-category-4b::0", "source": "acr_followup_guidelines", "title": "BI-RADS Assessment Categories 3, 4, and 5 — Follow-Up and Biopsy", "section": "BI-RADS Category 4: Suspicious > Category 4B", "text": "Category 4B means moderate suspicion for malignancy: above 10% and up to 50%. A benign biopsy result needs careful correlation with the imaging to decide whether it is concordant.", "index_text": "BI-RADS Assessment Categories 3, 4, and 5 — Follow-Up and Biopsy > BI-RADS Category 4: Suspicious > Category 4B: Category 4B means moderate suspicion for malignancy: above 10% and up to 50%. A benign biopsy result needs careful correlation with the imaging to decide whether it is concordant."}
+{"chunk_id": "acr_followup_guidelines::bi-rads-category-4-suspicious-category-4c::0", "source": "acr_followup_guidelines", "title": "BI-RADS Assessment Categories 3, 4, and 5 — Follow-Up and Biopsy", "section": "BI-RADS Category 4: Suspicious > Category 4C", "text": "Category 4C means high suspicion for malignancy: above 50% and below 95%. A malignant result is expected, so a benign result is usually treated as discordant unless it specifically explains the imaging finding.", "index_text": "BI-RADS Assessment Categories 3, 4, and 5 — Follow-Up and Biopsy > BI-RADS Category 4: Suspicious > Category 4C: Category 4C means high suspicion for malignancy: above 50% and below 95%. A malignant result is expected, so a benign result is usually treated as discordant unless it specifically explains the imaging finding."}
+{"chunk_id": "acr_followup_guidelines::bi-rads-category-5-highly-suggestive-of-malignancy::0", "source": "acr_followup_guidelines", "title": "BI-RADS Assessment Categories 3, 4, and 5 — Follow-Up and Biopsy", "section": "BI-RADS Category 5: Highly Suggestive of Malignancy", "text": "Category 5 means a likelihood of malignancy of 95% or more, and a tissue diagnosis is required. A non-malignant result from a needle biopsy is typically considered discordant. Classic examples are an irregular, spiculated, high-density mass, or new fine linear-branching calcifications in a segmental distribution.", "index_text": "BI-RADS Assessment Categories 3, 4, and 5 — Follow-Up and Biopsy > BI-RADS Category 5: Highly Suggestive of Malignancy: Category 5 means a likelihood of malignancy of 95% or more, and a tissue diagnosis is required. A non-malignant result from a needle biopsy is typically considered discordant. Classic examples are an irregular, spiculated, high-density mass, or new fine linear-branching calcifications in a segmental distribution."}
+{"chunk_id": "acr_followup_guidelines::concordance-review::0", "source": "acr_followup_guidelines", "title": "BI-RADS Assessment Categories 3, 4, and 5 — Follow-Up and Biopsy", "section": "Concordance Review", "text": "Radiologic–pathologic concordance means that the biopsy result satisfactorily explains what was seen on imaging. For example, a fibroadenoma explains a well-defined oval mass. The radiologist who performed the biopsy determines concordance, documents it and communicates it. A discordant result needs a repeat image-guided biopsy or surgical excision. Follow-up after a benign concordant biopsy is individualised.", "index_text": "BI-RADS Assessment Categories 3, 4, and 5 — Follow-Up and Biopsy > Concordance Review: Radiologic–pathologic concordance means that the biopsy result satisfactorily explains what was seen on imaging. For example, a fibroadenoma explains a well-defined oval mass. The radiologist who performed the biopsy determines concordance, documents it and communicates it. A discordant result needs a repeat image-guided biopsy or surgical excision. Follow-up after a benign concordant biopsy is individualised."}
+{"chunk_id": "acr_followup_guidelines::what-happens-after-an-abnormal-mammogram::0", "source": "acr_followup_guidelines", "title": "BI-RADS Assessment Categories 3, 4, and 5 — Follow-Up and Biopsy", "section": "What Happens After an Abnormal Mammogram?", "text": "1. Additional diagnostic mammographic views or a breast ultrasound may be recommended to characterise the finding further.\n2. If a biopsy is recommended (category 4 or 5), an image-guided core needle biopsy is the usual first procedure.\n3. The pathology result is compared with the imaging to check that they agree (concordance).\n4. If cancer is confirmed, staging and treatment planning begin with the care team.\n5. If the biopsy shows a benign result that agrees with the imaging, follow-up imaging is scheduled as the radiologist advises.\n\nAn AI risk estimate does not replace this clinical workflow. A risk estimate of any level does not bypass the need for clinical assessment, imaging interpretation by a radiologist and, where indicated, biopsy.", "index_text": "BI-RADS Assessment Categories 3, 4, and 5 — Follow-Up and Biopsy > What Happens After an Abnormal Mammogram?: 1. Additional diagnostic mammographic views or a breast ultrasound may be recommended to characterise the finding further.\n2. If a biopsy is recommended (category 4 or 5), an image-guided core needle biopsy is the usual first procedure.\n3. The pathology result is compared with the imaging to check that they agree (concordance).\n4. If cancer is confirmed, staging and treatment planning begin with the care team.\n5. If the biopsy shows a benign result that agrees with the imaging, follow-up imaging is scheduled as the radiologist advises.\n\nAn AI risk estimate does not replace this clinical workflow. A risk estimate of any level does not bypass the need for clinical assessment, imaging interpretation by a radiologist and, where indicated, biopsy."}
+{"chunk_id": "birads_terminology::bi-rads-mass-descriptors-margin-shape-and-density::0", "source": "birads_terminology", "title": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density", "section": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density", "text": "BI-RADS is the American College of Radiology's lexicon for describing breast imaging findings. The inputs in this app use the terms of the 4th edition, which the UCI Mammographic Mass data were coded with. The 5th edition (2013) and the BI-RADS v2025 update renamed or removed some of them, as noted under each term. Percentages quoted below come from specific published series and vary between studies; they are context, not a risk estimate for an individual.", "index_text": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density: BI-RADS is the American College of Radiology's lexicon for describing breast imaging findings. The inputs in this app use the terms of the 4th edition, which the UCI Mammographic Mass data were coded with. The 5th edition (2013) and the BI-RADS v2025 update renamed or removed some of them, as noted under each term. Percentages quoted below come from specific published series and vary between studies; they are context, not a risk estimate for an individual."}
+{"chunk_id": "birads_terminology::mass-margin::0", "source": "birads_terminology", "title": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density", "section": "Mass Margin", "text": "Margin is the border of a mass on the mammogram. Circumscribed margins are reassuring, while indistinct (ill-defined) and spiculated margins are suspicious.", "index_text": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density > Mass Margin: Margin is the border of a mass on the mammogram. Circumscribed margins are reassuring, while indistinct (ill-defined) and spiculated margins are suspicious."}
+{"chunk_id": "birads_terminology::mass-margin-circumscribed-margin::0", "source": "birads_terminology", "title": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density", "section": "Mass Margin > Circumscribed Margin", "text": "A circumscribed margin is well defined: at least 75% of the border is sharply demarcated, with an abrupt transition between the mass and the surrounding tissue. Circumscribed masses are usually benign; examples include fibroadenomas and cysts. In a large study of non-palpable, non-calcified, circumscribed solid masses followed with mammography, 19 of 1,403 (1.4%) proved malignant. That figure applies to that specific group, not to every circumscribed mass.", "index_text": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density > Mass Margin > Circumscribed Margin: A circumscribed margin is well defined: at least 75% of the border is sharply demarcated, with an abrupt transition between the mass and the surrounding tissue. Circumscribed masses are usually benign; examples include fibroadenomas and cysts. In a large study of non-palpable, non-calcified, circumscribed solid masses followed with mammography, 19 of 1,403 (1.4%) proved malignant. That figure applies to that specific group, not to every circumscribed mass."}
+{"chunk_id": "birads_terminology::mass-margin-microlobulated-margin::0", "source": "birads_terminology", "title": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density", "section": "Mass Margin > Microlobulated Margin", "text": "A microlobulated margin has short-cycle undulations along the border of the mass. The 4th and 5th editions listed it as a suspicious descriptor. The BI-RADS v2025 update removed it to avoid confusion with the shape term \"lobulated\"; such a margin is now described as indistinct. Published probabilities of malignancy for this margin vary widely between series, so no single figure is given here.", "index_text": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density > Mass Margin > Microlobulated Margin: A microlobulated margin has short-cycle undulations along the border of the mass. The 4th and 5th editions listed it as a suspicious descriptor. The BI-RADS v2025 update removed it to avoid confusion with the shape term \"lobulated\"; such a margin is now described as indistinct. Published probabilities of malignancy for this margin vary widely between series, so no single figure is given here."}
+{"chunk_id": "birads_terminology::mass-margin-obscured-margin::0", "source": "birads_terminology", "title": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density", "section": "Mass Margin > Obscured Margin", "text": "An obscured margin is one where 25% or more of the border is hidden by overlapping normal fibroglandular tissue, while the visible part is circumscribed. Because the margin cannot be fully assessed, the mass needs further diagnostic work-up, such as additional mammographic views or ultrasound, before a final assessment.", "index_text": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density > Mass Margin > Obscured Margin: An obscured margin is one where 25% or more of the border is hidden by overlapping normal fibroglandular tissue, while the visible part is circumscribed. Because the margin cannot be fully assessed, the mass needs further diagnostic work-up, such as additional mammographic views or ultrasound, before a final assessment."}
+{"chunk_id": "birads_terminology::mass-margin-ill-defined-margin::0", "source": "birads_terminology", "title": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density", "section": "Mass Margin > Ill-defined Margin", "text": "An ill-defined margin, called \"indistinct\" since the 5th edition, has no clear demarcation between all or part of the mass and the surrounding tissue. It is a suspicious finding. In one biopsy series, 29 of 66 masses (44%) with this margin were malignant.", "index_text": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density > Mass Margin > Ill-defined Margin: An ill-defined margin, called \"indistinct\" since the 5th edition, has no clear demarcation between all or part of the mass and the surrounding tissue. It is a suspicious finding. In one biopsy series, 29 of 66 masses (44%) with this margin were malignant."}
+{"chunk_id": "birads_terminology::mass-margin-spiculated-margin::0", "source": "birads_terminology", "title": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density", "section": "Mass Margin > Spiculated Margin", "text": "A spiculated margin has thin lines radiating from the edge of the mass. It is one of the most suspicious features on a mammogram: in a large series of biopsied lesions, 81% of spiculated masses were malignant. A spiculated mass warrants a tissue diagnosis, although a minority turn out to be benign.", "index_text": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density > Mass Margin > Spiculated Margin: A spiculated margin has thin lines radiating from the edge of the mass. It is one of the most suspicious features on a mammogram: in a large series of biopsied lesions, 81% of spiculated masses were malignant. A spiculated mass warrants a tissue diagnosis, although a minority turn out to be benign."}
+{"chunk_id": "birads_terminology::mass-shape::0", "source": "birads_terminology", "title": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density", "section": "Mass Shape", "text": "Shape describes the outline of the mass.", "index_text": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density > Mass Shape: Shape describes the outline of the mass."}
+{"chunk_id": "birads_terminology::mass-shape-round-shape::0", "source": "birads_terminology", "title": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density", "section": "Mass Shape > Round Shape", "text": "A round mass is spherical, ball-shaped or circular. Round masses are most often benign, such as cysts and fibroadenomas, particularly when the margin is circumscribed.", "index_text": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density > Mass Shape > Round Shape: A round mass is spherical, ball-shaped or circular. Round masses are most often benign, such as cysts and fibroadenomas, particularly when the margin is circumscribed."}
+{"chunk_id": "birads_terminology::mass-shape-oval-shape::0", "source": "birads_terminology", "title": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density", "section": "Mass Shape > Oval Shape", "text": "An oval mass is elliptical or egg-shaped, with one axis longer than the others. Oval shape, especially with a circumscribed margin, is generally reassuring.", "index_text": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density > Mass Shape > Oval Shape: An oval mass is elliptical or egg-shaped, with one axis longer than the others. Oval shape, especially with a circumscribed margin, is generally reassuring."}
+{"chunk_id": "birads_terminology::mass-shape-lobular-shape::0", "source": "birads_terminology", "title": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density", "section": "Mass Shape > Lobular Shape", "text": "\"Lobular\" is the 4th-edition term, used in the UCI data, for a mass with an undulating contour. The 5th edition removed it, and the BI-RADS v2025 update brought it back as \"lobulated\": a mass with one or more indentations that give it an undulating contour. Lobulated masses are usually benign but are considered slightly more suspicious than oval ones.", "index_text": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density > Mass Shape > Lobular Shape: \"Lobular\" is the 4th-edition term, used in the UCI data, for a mass with an undulating contour. The 5th edition removed it, and the BI-RADS v2025 update brought it back as \"lobulated\": a mass with one or more indentations that give it an undulating contour. Lobulated masses are usually benign but are considered slightly more suspicious than oval ones."}
+{"chunk_id": "birads_terminology::mass-shape-irregular-shape::0", "source": "birads_terminology", "title": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density", "section": "Mass Shape > Irregular Shape", "text": "An irregular mass is neither round, oval nor lobulated. Irregular shape is a suspicious feature: in one series of biopsied lesions, 73% of irregular masses were malignant. An irregular, spiculated, high-density mass is a classic appearance of a BI-RADS category 5 finding.", "index_text": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density > Mass Shape > Irregular Shape: An irregular mass is neither round, oval nor lobulated. Irregular shape is a suspicious feature: in one series of biopsied lesions, 73% of irregular masses were malignant. An irregular, spiculated, high-density mass is a classic appearance of a BI-RADS category 5 finding."}
+{"chunk_id": "birads_terminology::mass-density::0", "source": "birads_terminology", "title": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density", "section": "Mass Density", "text": "Density compares how much the mass attenuates X-rays with an equal volume of fibroglandular tissue.", "index_text": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density > Mass Density: Density compares how much the mass attenuates X-rays with an equal volume of fibroglandular tissue."}
+{"chunk_id": "birads_terminology::mass-density-high-density::0", "source": "birads_terminology", "title": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density", "section": "Mass Density > High Density", "text": "A high-density mass attenuates more X-rays than the same volume of fibroglandular tissue. High density is associated with malignancy: in one biopsy series, 70.2% of high-density masses were malignant, compared with 22.3% of iso-dense or low-density masses.", "index_text": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density > Mass Density > High Density: A high-density mass attenuates more X-rays than the same volume of fibroglandular tissue. High density is associated with malignancy: in one biopsy series, 70.2% of high-density masses were malignant, compared with 22.3% of iso-dense or low-density masses."}
+{"chunk_id": "birads_terminology::mass-density-iso-density::0", "source": "birads_terminology", "title": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density", "section": "Mass Density > Iso-density", "text": "An iso-dense mass, described as \"equal density\" in current terminology, has the same attenuation as fibroglandular tissue. On its own, iso-density neither indicates nor excludes malignancy.", "index_text": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density > Mass Density > Iso-density: An iso-dense mass, described as \"equal density\" in current terminology, has the same attenuation as fibroglandular tissue. On its own, iso-density neither indicates nor excludes malignancy."}
+{"chunk_id": "birads_terminology::mass-density-low-density::0", "source": "birads_terminology", "title": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density", "section": "Mass Density > Low Density", "text": "A low-density mass attenuates fewer X-rays than fibroglandular tissue but contains no fat. Low density on its own does not establish that a mass is benign.", "index_text": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density > Mass Density > Low Density: A low-density mass attenuates fewer X-rays than fibroglandular tissue but contains no fat. Low density on its own does not establish that a mass is benign."}
+{"chunk_id": "birads_terminology::mass-density-fat-containing::0", "source": "birads_terminology", "title": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density", "section": "Mass Density > Fat-containing", "text": "A fat-containing mass includes visible fat. Such masses, for example oil cysts, lipomas, galactoceles and hamartomas, almost always represent benign lesions. When the radiologist judges the appearance characteristic, the finding is assessed as BI-RADS category 2 (benign) and needs no further work-up.", "index_text": "BI-RADS Mass Descriptors — Margin, Shape, and Density > Mass Density > Fat-containing: A fat-containing mass includes visible fat. Such masses, for example oil cysts, lipomas, galactoceles and hamartomas, almost always represent benign lesions. When the radiologist judges the appearance characteristic, the finding is assessed as BI-RADS category 2 (benign) and needs no further work-up."}
+{"chunk_id": "breast_imaging_glossary::what-is-a-mammographic-mass::0", "source": "breast_imaging_glossary", "title": "Breast Imaging Glossary — Patient and Clinician Reference", "section": "What is a Mammographic Mass?", "text": "A mass is a three-dimensional finding with an outward-bulging contour, seen on two different mammographic projections (or on a single projection with tomosynthesis). Masses are described by their shape, margin and density. A finding seen on only one projection is called an asymmetry rather than a mass.", "index_text": "Breast Imaging Glossary — Patient and Clinician Reference > What is a Mammographic Mass?: A mass is a three-dimensional finding with an outward-bulging contour, seen on two different mammographic projections (or on a single projection with tomosynthesis). Masses are described by their shape, margin and density. A finding seen on only one projection is called an asymmetry rather than a mass."}
+{"chunk_id": "breast_imaging_glossary::what-is-a-mammogram::0", "source": "breast_imaging_glossary", "title": "Breast Imaging Glossary — Patient and Clinician Reference", "section": "What is a Mammogram?", "text": "A mammogram is an X-ray of the breast and the primary tool for breast cancer screening. Digital mammography records the X-rays electronically, so images can be enhanced and stored. Digital breast tomosynthesis (3D mammography) takes images from several angles and reconstructs thin slices through the breast. It improves cancer detection in many women with dense breasts, although a large US study found no gain at later screening rounds in extremely dense breasts.", "index_text": "Breast Imaging Glossary — Patient and Clinician Reference > What is a Mammogram?: A mammogram is an X-ray of the breast and the primary tool for breast cancer screening. Digital mammography records the X-rays electronically, so images can be enhanced and stored. Digital breast tomosynthesis (3D mammography) takes images from several angles and reconstructs thin slices through the breast. It improves cancer detection in many women with dense breasts, although a large US study found no gain at later screening rounds in extremely dense breasts."}
+{"chunk_id": "breast_imaging_glossary::what-is-bi-rads::0", "source": "breast_imaging_glossary", "title": "Breast Imaging Glossary — Patient and Clinician Reference", "section": "What is BI-RADS?", "text": "BI-RADS (Breast Imaging Reporting and Data System) is the American College of Radiology's standardised lexicon, report structure, assessment-category scheme and audit system for mammography, ultrasound, MRI and contrast-enhanced mammography. Its final assessment categories are:\n- Category 0: Incomplete — additional imaging and/or comparison with prior images is needed\n- Category 1: Negative — routine screening\n- Category 2: Benign — routine screening\n- Category 3: Probably benign — short-interval follow-up, usually at 6 months\n- Category 4: Suspicious — tissue diagnosis (subdivided 4A, 4B, 4C)\n- Category 5: Highly suggestive of malignancy — tissue diagnosis\n- Category 6: Known biopsy-proven malignancy", "index_text": "Breast Imaging Glossary — Patient and Clinician Reference > What is BI-RADS?: BI-RADS (Breast Imaging Reporting and Data System) is the American College of Radiology's standardised lexicon, report structure, assessment-category scheme and audit system for mammography, ultrasound, MRI and contrast-enhanced mammography. Its final assessment categories are:\n- Category 0: Incomplete — additional imaging and/or comparison with prior images is needed\n- Category 1: Negative — routine screening\n- Category 2: Benign — routine screening\n- Category 3: Probably benign — short-interval follow-up, usually at 6 months\n- Category 4: Suspicious — tissue diagnosis (subdivided 4A, 4B, 4C)\n- Category 5: Highly suggestive of malignancy — tissue diagnosis\n- Category 6: Known biopsy-proven malignancy"}
+{"chunk_id": "breast_imaging_glossary::what-is-a-fibroadenoma::0", "source": "breast_imaging_glossary", "title": "Breast Imaging Glossary — Patient and Clinician Reference", "section": "What is a Fibroadenoma?", "text": "A fibroadenoma is the most common benign breast tumour, typically seen as a well-defined, oval or round, circumscribed mass in young women. Coarse calcifications are typical of older, involuting fibroadenomas. Removal is considered when a fibroadenoma is large, growing, causing symptoms, or looks atypical on imaging.", "index_text": "Breast Imaging Glossary — Patient and Clinician Reference > What is a Fibroadenoma?: A fibroadenoma is the most common benign breast tumour, typically seen as a well-defined, oval or round, circumscribed mass in young women. Coarse calcifications are typical of older, involuting fibroadenomas. Removal is considered when a fibroadenoma is large, growing, causing symptoms, or looks atypical on imaging."}
+{"chunk_id": "breast_imaging_glossary::what-is-a-cyst::0", "source": "breast_imaging_glossary", "title": "Breast Imaging Glossary — Patient and Clinician Reference", "section": "What is a Cyst?", "text": "A breast cyst is a fluid-filled sac. On ultrasound, a simple cyst is circumscribed, round or oval, free of internal echoes (anechoic) and thin-walled, often with posterior acoustic enhancement; it is benign. Complicated cysts are also usually benign and may be aspirated if they cause symptoms. Complex cystic and solid lesions should be biopsied.", "index_text": "Breast Imaging Glossary — Patient and Clinician Reference > What is a Cyst?: A breast cyst is a fluid-filled sac. On ultrasound, a simple cyst is circumscribed, round or oval, free of internal echoes (anechoic) and thin-walled, often with posterior acoustic enhancement; it is benign. Complicated cysts are also usually benign and may be aspirated if they cause symptoms. Complex cystic and solid lesions should be biopsied."}
+{"chunk_id": "breast_imaging_glossary::what-is-fine-needle-aspiration-fna::0", "source": "breast_imaging_glossary", "title": "Breast Imaging Glossary — Patient and Clinician Reference", "section": "What is Fine Needle Aspiration (FNA)?", "text": "Fine needle aspiration is a minimally invasive procedure in which a thin needle draws cells from a mass for cytological examination. The Wisconsin Diagnostic Breast Cancer (WDBC) dataset's features were computed from digitised FNA images: 10 nuclear measurements, each summarised by its mean, standard error and worst value, giving 30 features.", "index_text": "Breast Imaging Glossary — Patient and Clinician Reference > What is Fine Needle Aspiration (FNA)?: Fine needle aspiration is a minimally invasive procedure in which a thin needle draws cells from a mass for cytological examination. The Wisconsin Diagnostic Breast Cancer (WDBC) dataset's features were computed from digitised FNA images: 10 nuclear measurements, each summarised by its mean, standard error and worst value, giving 30 features."}
+{"chunk_id": "breast_imaging_glossary::what-is-a-biopsy::0", "source": "breast_imaging_glossary", "title": "Breast Imaging Glossary — Patient and Clinician Reference", "section": "What is a Biopsy?", "text": "A breast biopsy removes a tissue sample from an abnormality for examination by a pathologist. An image-guided core needle biopsy is the preferred first procedure for a suspicious finding. A biopsy can confirm cancer; a benign result is reliable only when it agrees with the imaging findings (concordance). An AI risk estimate is not a substitute for a biopsy.", "index_text": "Breast Imaging Glossary — Patient and Clinician Reference > What is a Biopsy?: A breast biopsy removes a tissue sample from an abnormality for examination by a pathologist. An image-guided core needle biopsy is the preferred first procedure for a suspicious finding. A biopsy can confirm cancer; a benign result is reliable only when it agrees with the imaging findings (concordance). An AI risk estimate is not a substitute for a biopsy."}
+{"chunk_id": "breast_imaging_glossary::what-are-nuclear-features::0", "source": "breast_imaging_glossary", "title": "Breast Imaging Glossary — Patient and Clinician Reference", "section": "What are Nuclear Features?", "text": "Nuclear features describe the cell nuclei seen under the microscope, such as radius, texture, perimeter, area, smoothness, compactness, concavity, concave points, symmetry and fractal dimension. The WDBC dataset contains 30 such features.", "index_text": "Breast Imaging Glossary — Patient and Clinician Reference > What are Nuclear Features?: Nuclear features describe the cell nuclei seen under the microscope, such as radius, texture, perimeter, area, smoothness, compactness, concavity, concave points, symmetry and fractal dimension. The WDBC dataset contains 30 such features."}
+{"chunk_id": "breast_imaging_glossary::what-is-malignancy-probability::0", "source": "breast_imaging_glossary", "title": "Breast Imaging Glossary — Patient and Clinician Reference", "section": "What is Malignancy Probability?", "text": "A malignancy probability is a model's estimate of how often masses with the same features turned out to be cancer, expressed as a percentage. It reflects the cases the model learned from: in the UCI Mammographic Mass data, 445 of 961 masses were malignant, a far higher share than among masses found at routine screening. A probability is a statistical estimate, not a diagnosis.", "index_text": "Breast Imaging Glossary — Patient and Clinician Reference > What is Malignancy Probability?: A malignancy probability is a model's estimate of how often masses with the same features turned out to be cancer, expressed as a percentage. It reflects the cases the model learned from: in the UCI Mammographic Mass data, 445 of 961 masses were malignant, a far higher share than among masses found at routine screening. A probability is a statistical estimate, not a diagnosis."}
+{"chunk_id": "breast_imaging_glossary::what-is-sensitivity-and-specificity::0", "source": "breast_imaging_glossary", "title": "Breast Imaging Glossary — Patient and Clinician Reference", "section": "What is Sensitivity and Specificity?", "text": "Sensitivity is the share of people with the disease whom a test correctly flags: true positives divided by all people with the disease. Specificity is the share of people without the disease whom the test correctly clears: true negatives divided by all people without it. Screening programmes trade one against the other, and most women recalled after a screening mammogram do not have cancer.", "index_text": "Breast Imaging Glossary — Patient and Clinician Reference > What is Sensitivity and Specificity?: Sensitivity is the share of people with the disease whom a test correctly flags: true positives divided by all people with the disease. Specificity is the share of people without the disease whom the test correctly clears: true negatives divided by all people without it. Screening programmes trade one against the other, and most women recalled after a screening mammogram do not have cancer."}
+{"chunk_id": "imaging_modalities::mammography-how-mammography-works::0", "source": "imaging_modalities", "title": "Breast Imaging Modalities Compared", "section": "Mammography > How Mammography Works", "text": "A mammogram is a low-dose X-ray of the compressed breast, taken in two views of each breast. Full-field digital mammography records the image electronically. Digital breast tomosynthesis (DBT, \"3D mammography\") takes several low-dose exposures from different angles and reconstructs thin slices, which reduces the overlap of tissue that hides or mimics masses on a 2D image.", "index_text": "Breast Imaging Modalities Compared > Mammography > How Mammography Works: A mammogram is a low-dose X-ray of the compressed breast, taken in two views of each breast. Full-field digital mammography records the image electronically. Digital breast tomosynthesis (DBT, \"3D mammography\") takes several low-dose exposures from different angles and reconstructs thin slices, which reduces the overlap of tissue that hides or mimics masses on a 2D image."}
+{"chunk_id": "imaging_modalities::mammography-mammography-accuracy::0", "source": "imaging_modalities", "title": "Breast Imaging Modalities Compared", "section": "Mammography > Mammography Accuracy", "text": "In US screening data, mammography finds roughly 80–87% of the cancers present (sensitivity), with a specificity of about 89%. Sensitivity is lower in dense breasts, by 10–29 percentage points across all ages. About 10% of screened women are recalled for more tests, about 5 cancers are found per 1,000 screens, and only about 4–5% of recalled women turn out to have cancer. Over ten years of annual screening, about half of US women have at least one false-positive result, and 7–17% of these have a biopsy.", "index_text": "Breast Imaging Modalities Compared > Mammography > Mammography Accuracy: In US screening data, mammography finds roughly 80–87% of the cancers present (sensitivity), with a specificity of about 89%. Sensitivity is lower in dense breasts, by 10–29 percentage points across all ages. About 10% of screened women are recalled for more tests, about 5 cancers are found per 1,000 screens, and only about 4–5% of recalled women turn out to have cancer. Over ten years of annual screening, about half of US women have at least one false-positive result, and 7–17% of these have a biopsy."}
+{"chunk_id": "imaging_modalities::mammography-tomosynthesis::0", "source": "imaging_modalities", "title": "Breast Imaging Modalities Compared", "section": "Mammography > Tomosynthesis", "text": "In a large US study comparing practices before and after adopting DBT, adding tomosynthesis lowered the recall rate from 107 to 91 per 1,000 screens and raised the cancer detection rate from 4.2 to 5.4 per 1,000, with most of the gain in invasive cancers. Whether DBT reduces breast cancer deaths compared with 2D mammography is not yet known and is being tested in the TMIST trial. NCCN recommends screening mammography with tomosynthesis.", "index_text": "Breast Imaging Modalities Compared > Mammography > Tomosynthesis: In a large US study comparing practices before and after adopting DBT, adding tomosynthesis lowered the recall rate from 107 to 91 per 1,000 screens and raised the cancer detection rate from 4.2 to 5.4 per 1,000, with most of the gain in invasive cancers. Whether DBT reduces breast cancer deaths compared with 2D mammography is not yet known and is being tested in the TMIST trial. NCCN recommends screening mammography with tomosynthesis."}
+{"chunk_id": "imaging_modalities::mammography-mammography-strengths::0", "source": "imaging_modalities", "title": "Breast Imaging Modalities Compared", "section": "Mammography > Mammography Strengths", "text": "Mammography is the only screening test shown in randomised trials to reduce breast cancer mortality. It is widely available, standardised through BI-RADS, and detects microcalcifications, an early sign of ductal carcinoma in situ that other modalities often miss.", "index_text": "Breast Imaging Modalities Compared > Mammography > Mammography Strengths: Mammography is the only screening test shown in randomised trials to reduce breast cancer mortality. It is widely available, standardised through BI-RADS, and detects microcalcifications, an early sign of ductal carcinoma in situ that other modalities often miss."}
+{"chunk_id": "imaging_modalities::mammography-mammography-limitations::0", "source": "imaging_modalities", "title": "Breast Imaging Modalities Compared", "section": "Mammography > Mammography Limitations", "text": "It uses ionising radiation, although the dose is small: about 0.28 mSv for a screening digital mammogram and 0.34 mSv for tomosynthesis, equivalent to roughly one to one and a half months of natural background radiation. The National Cancer Institute estimates that, in theory, annual mammograms between ages 40 and 80 could cause up to one breast cancer per 1,000 women, a risk weighed against the deaths that screening prevents. Sensitivity falls in dense breasts, compression is uncomfortable for some women, and overlapping tissue on 2D images causes both missed cancers and false recalls.", "index_text": "Breast Imaging Modalities Compared > Mammography > Mammography Limitations: It uses ionising radiation, although the dose is small: about 0.28 mSv for a screening digital mammogram and 0.34 mSv for tomosynthesis, equivalent to roughly one to one and a half months of natural background radiation. The National Cancer Institute estimates that, in theory, annual mammograms between ages 40 and 80 could cause up to one breast cancer per 1,000 women, a risk weighed against the deaths that screening prevents. Sensitivity falls in dense breasts, compression is uncomfortable for some women, and overlapping tissue on 2D images causes both missed cancers and false recalls."}
+{"chunk_id": "imaging_modalities::breast-ultrasound-how-ultrasound-works::0", "source": "imaging_modalities", "title": "Breast Imaging Modalities Compared", "section": "Breast Ultrasound > How Ultrasound Works", "text": "Ultrasound uses high-frequency sound waves to produce real-time images without radiation. It distinguishes fluid-filled cysts from solid masses and shows the margins, shape and orientation of a mass.", "index_text": "Breast Imaging Modalities Compared > Breast Ultrasound > How Ultrasound Works: Ultrasound uses high-frequency sound waves to produce real-time images without radiation. It distinguishes fluid-filled cysts from solid masses and shows the margins, shape and orientation of a mass."}
+{"chunk_id": "imaging_modalities::breast-ultrasound-ultrasound-as-supplemental-screening::0", "source": "imaging_modalities", "title": "Breast Imaging Modalities Compared", "section": "Breast Ultrasound > Ultrasound as Supplemental Screening", "text": "In women with dense breasts, adding screening ultrasound to mammography finds additional cancers but at the cost of many more false positives. In the ACRIN 6666 trial, ultrasound found 4.2 extra cancers per 1,000 women in the first round, while only 8.9% of the biopsies it prompted showed cancer, compared with 22.6% for mammography. In routine US practice the extra yield was smaller, about 1.3 cancers per 1,000 in the first year. The USPSTF finds the evidence insufficient to recommend for or against supplemental ultrasound or MRI for dense breasts alone.", "index_text": "Breast Imaging Modalities Compared > Breast Ultrasound > Ultrasound as Supplemental Screening: In women with dense breasts, adding screening ultrasound to mammography finds additional cancers but at the cost of many more false positives. In the ACRIN 6666 trial, ultrasound found 4.2 extra cancers per 1,000 women in the first round, while only 8.9% of the biopsies it prompted showed cancer, compared with 22.6% for mammography. In routine US practice the extra yield was smaller, about 1.3 cancers per 1,000 in the first year. The USPSTF finds the evidence insufficient to recommend for or against supplemental ultrasound or MRI for dense breasts alone."}
+{"chunk_id": "imaging_modalities::breast-ultrasound-ultrasound-strengths-and-limitations::0", "source": "imaging_modalities", "title": "Breast Imaging Modalities Compared", "section": "Breast Ultrasound > Ultrasound Strengths and Limitations", "text": "Ultrasound involves no radiation, is safe in pregnancy, guides needle biopsies in real time and is the first test for a palpable lump in women under 30. It cannot reliably show microcalcifications, its quality depends on the operator, and its false-positive rate is higher than mammography's.", "index_text": "Breast Imaging Modalities Compared > Breast Ultrasound > Ultrasound Strengths and Limitations: Ultrasound involves no radiation, is safe in pregnancy, guides needle biopsies in real time and is the first test for a palpable lump in women under 30. It cannot reliably show microcalcifications, its quality depends on the operator, and its false-positive rate is higher than mammography's."}
+{"chunk_id": "imaging_modalities::breast-mri-how-breast-mri-works::0", "source": "imaging_modalities", "title": "Breast Imaging Modalities Compared", "section": "Breast MRI > How Breast MRI Works", "text": "Breast MRI uses a magnetic field and radio waves with an intravenous gadolinium contrast agent. Cancers are detected mainly by their contrast enhancement, reflecting their blood supply, together with their morphology.", "index_text": "Breast Imaging Modalities Compared > Breast MRI > How Breast MRI Works: Breast MRI uses a magnetic field and radio waves with an intravenous gadolinium contrast agent. Cancers are detected mainly by their contrast enhancement, reflecting their blood supply, together with their morphology."}
+{"chunk_id": "imaging_modalities::breast-mri-mri-accuracy::0", "source": "imaging_modalities", "title": "Breast Imaging Modalities Compared", "section": "Breast MRI > MRI Accuracy", "text": "MRI is the most sensitive breast imaging test and detects cancers missed by mammography and ultrasound, particularly in dense breasts and in high-risk women. Its specificity is lower, so it leads to more recalls and biopsies. In the Dutch DENSE trial, offering MRI to women with extremely dense breasts after a normal mammogram cut the rate of interval cancers from 5.0 to 2.5 per 1,000 women invited (0.8 per 1,000 among those actually screened), at the cost of about 80 false-positive results per 1,000 MRI screens.", "index_text": "Breast Imaging Modalities Compared > Breast MRI > MRI Accuracy: MRI is the most sensitive breast imaging test and detects cancers missed by mammography and ultrasound, particularly in dense breasts and in high-risk women. Its specificity is lower, so it leads to more recalls and biopsies. In the Dutch DENSE trial, offering MRI to women with extremely dense breasts after a normal mammogram cut the rate of interval cancers from 5.0 to 2.5 per 1,000 women invited (0.8 per 1,000 among those actually screened), at the cost of about 80 false-positive results per 1,000 MRI screens."}
+{"chunk_id": "imaging_modalities::breast-mri-when-mri-is-recommended::0", "source": "imaging_modalities", "title": "Breast Imaging Modalities Compared", "section": "Breast MRI > When MRI Is Recommended", "text": "Annual MRI alongside mammography is recommended for women at high risk: a lifetime risk of about 20–25% or greater by a family-history model, a BRCA1 or BRCA2 mutation (or an untested first-degree relative of a carrier), or chest radiation before age 30. Under NCCN guidance, MRI for women at increased risk may begin at 25, and for women with extremely dense breasts and no other risk factor it can be considered from age 50 (possibly 40). MRI is also used to assess the extent of a known cancer before surgery, to monitor the response to neoadjuvant therapy and to check implants.", "index_text": "Breast Imaging Modalities Compared > Breast MRI > When MRI Is Recommended: Annual MRI alongside mammography is recommended for women at high risk: a lifetime risk of about 20–25% or greater by a family-history model, a BRCA1 or BRCA2 mutation (or an untested first-degree relative of a carrier), or chest radiation before age 30. Under NCCN guidance, MRI for women at increased risk may begin at 25, and for women with extremely dense breasts and no other risk factor it can be considered from age 50 (possibly 40). MRI is also used to assess the extent of a known cancer before surgery, to monitor the response to neoadjuvant therapy and to check implants."}
+{"chunk_id": "imaging_modalities::breast-mri-mri-limitations::0", "source": "imaging_modalities", "title": "Breast Imaging Modalities Compared", "section": "Breast MRI > MRI Limitations", "text": "MRI is expensive, takes longer than mammography, requires an intravenous contrast agent, and is not suitable for everyone (for example because of some implanted devices, severe claustrophobia or pregnancy). It does not show microcalcifications. Contrast-enhanced mammography, molecular breast imaging or whole-breast ultrasound are alternatives when MRI is unavailable or cannot be done.", "index_text": "Breast Imaging Modalities Compared > Breast MRI > MRI Limitations: MRI is expensive, takes longer than mammography, requires an intravenous contrast agent, and is not suitable for everyone (for example because of some implanted devices, severe claustrophobia or pregnancy). It does not show microcalcifications. Contrast-enhanced mammography, molecular breast imaging or whole-breast ultrasound are alternatives when MRI is unavailable or cannot be done."}
+{"chunk_id": "imaging_modalities::comparison-summary::0", "source": "imaging_modalities", "title": "Breast Imaging Modalities Compared", "section": "Comparison Summary", "text": "For routine screening, mammography (preferably with tomosynthesis) is the standard. For women at high risk, annual MRI is added. For dense breasts without other risk factors, supplemental ultrasound or MRI finds more cancers but with more false positives, and guidelines differ on whether to recommend it. For a palpable lump in a young woman, ultrasound is the first test. Imaging findings are described with the BI-RADS lexicon, and an assessment category (0–6) sets the follow-up.", "index_text": "Breast Imaging Modalities Compared > Comparison Summary: For routine screening, mammography (preferably with tomosynthesis) is the standard. For women at high risk, annual MRI is added. For dense breasts without other risk factors, supplemental ultrasound or MRI finds more cancers but with more false positives, and guidelines differ on whether to recommend it. For a palpable lump in a young woman, ultrasound is the first test. Imaging findings are described with the BI-RADS lexicon, and an assessment category (0–6) sets the follow-up."}
+{"chunk_id": "nccn_screening_guidelines::overview::0", "source": "nccn_screening_guidelines", "title": "Breast Cancer Screening Guidelines — NCCN, ACS and USPSTF", "section": "Overview", "text": "Three US bodies publish widely used breast cancer screening guidelines: the National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN), the American Cancer Society (ACS) and the US Preventive Services Task Force (USPSTF). They agree that average-risk women should have regular screening mammography from their forties and that women at high risk need earlier and more intensive screening, but they differ on the starting age, the interval and the role of supplemental imaging.", "index_text": "Breast Cancer Screening Guidelines — NCCN, ACS and USPSTF > Overview: Three US bodies publish widely used breast cancer screening guidelines: the National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN), the American Cancer Society (ACS) and the US Preventive Services Task Force (USPSTF). They agree that average-risk women should have regular screening mammography from their forties and that women at high risk need earlier and more intensive screening, but they differ on the starting age, the interval and the role of supplemental imaging."}
+{"chunk_id": "nccn_screening_guidelines::nccn-average-risk-recommendations::0", "source": "nccn_screening_guidelines", "title": "Breast Cancer Screening Guidelines — NCCN, ACS and USPSTF", "section": "NCCN Average-Risk Recommendations", "text": "NCCN (Breast Cancer Screening and Diagnosis, version 1.2026) defines average risk as no known genetic predisposition and no family or personal history suggesting increased risk.\n\n**Ages 25–39**: A clinical encounter every one to three years that includes breast cancer risk assessment and a breast examination. Mammography is not recommended routinely.\n\n**Age 40 and over**: An annual clinical encounter with ongoing risk assessment and a breast examination, plus an annual screening mammogram with tomosynthesis.\n\n**Upper age limit**: There is none. Screening continues as long as the woman is healthy and expected to live more than ten years, unless she and her clinician decide otherwise.", "index_text": "Breast Cancer Screening Guidelines — NCCN, ACS and USPSTF > NCCN Average-Risk Recommendations: NCCN (Breast Cancer Screening and Diagnosis, version 1.2026) defines average risk as no known genetic predisposition and no family or personal history suggesting increased risk.\n\n**Ages 25–39**: A clinical encounter every one to three years that includes breast cancer risk assessment and a breast examination. Mammography is not recommended routinely.\n\n**Age 40 and over**: An annual clinical encounter with ongoing risk assessment and a breast examination, plus an annual screening mammogram with tomosynthesis.\n\n**Upper age limit**: There is none. Screening continues as long as the woman is healthy and expected to live more than ten years, unless she and her clinician decide otherwise."}
+{"chunk_id": "nccn_screening_guidelines::nccn-increased-risk-recommendations::0", "source": "nccn_screening_guidelines", "title": "Breast Cancer Screening Guidelines — NCCN, ACS and USPSTF", "section": "NCCN Increased-Risk Recommendations", "text": "NCCN recommends a breast cancer risk assessment by age 25. Women in any of the following groups see their clinician every six to twelve months and follow the schedule for their group.\n\n**Residual lifetime risk of 20% or more** (calculated with a model based on comprehensive family history, such as BRCAPRO, Tyrer-Cuzick, BOADICEA/CanRisk or the BCSC model): annual mammography with tomosynthesis may begin as early as 30 and no later than 40; annual breast MRI may begin as early as 25 and no later than 40.\n\n**Radiation therapy to the chest or breast between ages 10 and 30**: screening starts eight years after the radiation ended, but not before 25, with an annual mammogram with tomosynthesis and an annual breast MRI.", "index_text": "Breast Cancer Screening Guidelines — NCCN, ACS and USPSTF > NCCN Increased-Risk Recommendations: NCCN recommends a breast cancer risk assessment by age 25. Women in any of the following groups see their clinician every six to twelve months and follow the schedule for their group.\n\n**Residual lifetime risk of 20% or more** (calculated with a model based on comprehensive family history, such as BRCAPRO, Tyrer-Cuzick, BOADICEA/CanRisk or the BCSC model): annual mammography with tomosynthesis may begin as early as 30 and no later than 40; annual breast MRI may begin as early as 25 and no later than 40.\n\n**Radiation therapy to the chest or breast between ages 10 and 30**: screening starts eight years after the radiation ended, but not before 25, with an annual mammogram with tomosynthesis and an annual breast MRI."}
+{"chunk_id": "nccn_screening_guidelines::nccn-increased-risk-recommendations::1", "source": "nccn_screening_guidelines", "title": "Breast Cancer Screening Guidelines — NCCN, ACS and USPSTF", "section": "NCCN Increased-Risk Recommendations", "text": "**Five-year risk of invasive breast cancer of 1.7% or more** (Gail model at age 35 or over, or an imaging-based model): annual mammography with tomosynthesis and consideration of annual MRI.\n\n**Atypical ductal hyperplasia, lobular carcinoma in situ or atypical lobular hyperplasia**: annual mammography from diagnosis but not before 30, and consideration of annual MRI from diagnosis but not before 25.\n\n**Dense breasts with no other risk factor**: annual mammography with tomosynthesis starting no later than 40 (not before 30). For heterogeneously dense breasts, supplemental tests are discussed individually; for extremely dense breasts, breast MRI is recommended from age 50 and possibly from 40.\n\nWhen MRI is unavailable or cannot be done, contrast-enhanced mammography, molecular breast imaging or whole-breast ultrasound are alternatives.", "index_text": "Breast Cancer Screening Guidelines — NCCN, ACS and USPSTF > NCCN Increased-Risk Recommendations: **Five-year risk of invasive breast cancer of 1.7% or more** (Gail model at age 35 or over, or an imaging-based model): annual mammography with tomosynthesis and consideration of annual MRI.\n\n**Atypical ductal hyperplasia, lobular carcinoma in situ or atypical lobular hyperplasia**: annual mammography from diagnosis but not before 30, and consideration of annual MRI from diagnosis but not before 25.\n\n**Dense breasts with no other risk factor**: annual mammography with tomosynthesis starting no later than 40 (not before 30). For heterogeneously dense breasts, supplemental tests are discussed individually; for extremely dense breasts, breast MRI is recommended from age 50 and possibly from 40.\n\nWhen MRI is unavailable or cannot be done, contrast-enhanced mammography, molecular breast imaging or whole-breast ultrasound are alternatives."}
+{"chunk_id": "nccn_screening_guidelines::genetic-risk-factors::0", "source": "nccn_screening_guidelines", "title": "Breast Cancer Screening Guidelines — NCCN, ACS and USPSTF", "section": "Genetic Risk Factors", "text": "A personal or family history suggesting a hereditary predisposition (for example BRCA1, BRCA2, TP53, PTEN, CDH1, PALB2, ATM or CHEK2) leads to referral for genetic counselling and testing, and screening then follows NCCN's guideline for hereditary breast, ovarian, pancreatic and prostate cancers. In general, women at increased risk may start annual mammography at 30 and annual MRI at 25. BRCA1 carriers have a 55–72% lifetime risk of breast cancer and BRCA2 carriers 45–69%.", "index_text": "Breast Cancer Screening Guidelines — NCCN, ACS and USPSTF > Genetic Risk Factors: A personal or family history suggesting a hereditary predisposition (for example BRCA1, BRCA2, TP53, PTEN, CDH1, PALB2, ATM or CHEK2) leads to referral for genetic counselling and testing, and screening then follows NCCN's guideline for hereditary breast, ovarian, pancreatic and prostate cancers. In general, women at increased risk may start annual mammography at 30 and annual MRI at 25. BRCA1 carriers have a 55–72% lifetime risk of breast cancer and BRCA2 carriers 45–69%."}
+{"chunk_id": "nccn_screening_guidelines::acs-recommendations::0", "source": "nccn_screening_guidelines", "title": "Breast Cancer Screening Guidelines — NCCN, ACS and USPSTF", "section": "ACS Recommendations", "text": "For average-risk women, the ACS recommends that women aged 40–44 have the option to start annual mammography; women aged 45–54 have a mammogram every year; and women 55 and older switch to every other year or continue yearly, for as long as they are in good health and expected to live at least ten more years.\n\nFor high-risk women, the ACS recommends an annual breast MRI and mammogram, typically from age 30, for those with a lifetime risk of about 20–25% or greater by a family-history model, a known BRCA1 or BRCA2 mutation, a first-degree relative with such a mutation, chest radiation before age 30, or a hereditary syndrome such as Li-Fraumeni or Cowden syndrome. The ACS recommends against MRI screening when lifetime risk is below 15%.", "index_text": "Breast Cancer Screening Guidelines — NCCN, ACS and USPSTF > ACS Recommendations: For average-risk women, the ACS recommends that women aged 40–44 have the option to start annual mammography; women aged 45–54 have a mammogram every year; and women 55 and older switch to every other year or continue yearly, for as long as they are in good health and expected to live at least ten more years.\n\nFor high-risk women, the ACS recommends an annual breast MRI and mammogram, typically from age 30, for those with a lifetime risk of about 20–25% or greater by a family-history model, a known BRCA1 or BRCA2 mutation, a first-degree relative with such a mutation, chest radiation before age 30, or a hereditary syndrome such as Li-Fraumeni or Cowden syndrome. The ACS recommends against MRI screening when lifetime risk is below 15%."}
+{"chunk_id": "nccn_screening_guidelines::uspstf-recommendations::0", "source": "nccn_screening_guidelines", "title": "Breast Cancer Screening Guidelines — NCCN, ACS and USPSTF", "section": "USPSTF Recommendations", "text": "The 2024 USPSTF recommendation is biennial screening mammography for all women aged 40–74 (grade B). It finds the evidence insufficient to assess screening in women 75 and older, and insufficient to recommend for or against supplemental ultrasound or MRI for women with dense breasts and an otherwise negative mammogram.", "index_text": "Breast Cancer Screening Guidelines — NCCN, ACS and USPSTF > USPSTF Recommendations: The 2024 USPSTF recommendation is biennial screening mammography for all women aged 40–74 (grade B). It finds the evidence insufficient to assess screening in women 75 and older, and insufficient to recommend for or against supplemental ultrasound or MRI for women with dense breasts and an otherwise negative mammogram."}
+{"chunk_id": "nccn_screening_guidelines::risk-assessment-tools::0", "source": "nccn_screening_guidelines", "title": "Breast Cancer Screening Guidelines — NCCN, ACS and USPSTF", "section": "Risk Assessment Tools", "text": "**Gail model (NCI Breast Cancer Risk Assessment Tool)**: Estimates five-year and lifetime risk of invasive breast cancer from age, age at first period, age at first live birth, the number of first-degree relatives with breast cancer, the number of previous breast biopsies and any atypical hyperplasia, with adjustment for race and ethnicity. It was developed in women aged 35–74 and should not be used for known mutation carriers, women with a history of breast cancer, DCIS or LCIS, or women who had chest radiation; it also does not use second-degree relatives or the age at which relatives were diagnosed.\n\n**Tyrer-Cuzick (IBIS) model**: Uses a comprehensive family history on both sides of the family together with hormonal and reproductive factors, body mass index and, in recent versions, mammographic density. NCCN lists it among the models that can define a 20% lifetime risk.", "index_text": "Breast Cancer Screening Guidelines — NCCN, ACS and USPSTF > Risk Assessment Tools: **Gail model (NCI Breast Cancer Risk Assessment Tool)**: Estimates five-year and lifetime risk of invasive breast cancer from age, age at first period, age at first live birth, the number of first-degree relatives with breast cancer, the number of previous breast biopsies and any atypical hyperplasia, with adjustment for race and ethnicity. It was developed in women aged 35–74 and should not be used for known mutation carriers, women with a history of breast cancer, DCIS or LCIS, or women who had chest radiation; it also does not use second-degree relatives or the age at which relatives were diagnosed.\n\n**Tyrer-Cuzick (IBIS) model**: Uses a comprehensive family history on both sides of the family together with hormonal and reproductive factors, body mass index and, in recent versions, mammographic density. NCCN lists it among the models that can define a 20% lifetime risk."}
+{"chunk_id": "nccn_screening_guidelines::risk-assessment-tools::1", "source": "nccn_screening_guidelines", "title": "Breast Cancer Screening Guidelines — NCCN, ACS and USPSTF", "section": "Risk Assessment Tools", "text": "**BRCAPRO and BOADICEA/CanRisk**: Estimate the probability of carrying a BRCA1 or BRCA2 mutation and the resulting cancer risks from the family history of breast and ovarian cancer.\n\n**BCSC model**: Combines age, race and ethnicity, family history, previous biopsies and mammographic density to estimate five-year risk.", "index_text": "Breast Cancer Screening Guidelines — NCCN, ACS and USPSTF > Risk Assessment Tools: **BRCAPRO and BOADICEA/CanRisk**: Estimate the probability of carrying a BRCA1 or BRCA2 mutation and the resulting cancer risks from the family history of breast and ovarian cancer.\n\n**BCSC model**: Combines age, race and ethnicity, family history, previous biopsies and mammographic density to estimate five-year risk."}
+{"chunk_id": "patient_communication::communicating-risk::0", "source": "patient_communication", "title": "Patient Communication in Breast Cancer Screening", "section": "Communicating Risk", "text": "Patients need to understand their risk without undue alarm or false reassurance. Use absolute risks and natural frequencies rather than relative risks: \"about 8 out of 100 women with this finding turn out to have cancer\" is clearer than \"your risk is doubled\", which says nothing about the starting point. State the time frame the risk refers to, and say what the number is based on.", "index_text": "Patient Communication in Breast Cancer Screening > Communicating Risk: Patients need to understand their risk without undue alarm or false reassurance. Use absolute risks and natural frequencies rather than relative risks: \"about 8 out of 100 women with this finding turn out to have cancer\" is clearer than \"your risk is doubled\", which says nothing about the starting point. State the time frame the risk refers to, and say what the number is based on."}
+{"chunk_id": "patient_communication::explaining-a-model-s-risk-estimate::0", "source": "patient_communication", "title": "Patient Communication in Breast Cancer Screening", "section": "Explaining a Model's Risk Estimate", "text": "An AI risk estimate is a statistic, not a diagnosis. A probability of 40% means that, among masses with the same descriptors in the data the model learned from, about 40 in 100 were malignant. The data behind this tool came from one institution's diagnostic cases, nearly half of which were malignant, so its probabilities are not screening-population rates. A prediction set that contains both \"benign\" and \"malignant\" means the model cannot separate the two for these inputs. Whatever the estimate, the next step is set by the radiologist's assessment and, where indicated, a biopsy.", "index_text": "Patient Communication in Breast Cancer Screening > Explaining a Model's Risk Estimate: An AI risk estimate is a statistic, not a diagnosis. A probability of 40% means that, among masses with the same descriptors in the data the model learned from, about 40 in 100 were malignant. The data behind this tool came from one institution's diagnostic cases, nearly half of which were malignant, so its probabilities are not screening-population rates. A prediction set that contains both \"benign\" and \"malignant\" means the model cannot separate the two for these inputs. Whatever the estimate, the next step is set by the radiologist's assessment and, where indicated, a biopsy."}
+{"chunk_id": "patient_communication::conveying-screening-results::0", "source": "patient_communication", "title": "Patient Communication in Breast Cancer Screening", "section": "Conveying Screening Results", "text": "**Normal results**: A normal mammogram is reassuring but does not rule out cancer. Mammography finds roughly 80–87% of cancers present, and fewer in dense breasts. Encourage continued screening at the recommended interval and prompt review of any new symptom.\n\n**Abnormal results**: Most abnormal screening mammograms are not cancer. About 10% of US screening mammograms lead to a recall for more imaging, and only about one in twenty recalled women has cancer. Explain that additional views or an ultrasound are a routine part of screening, not a diagnosis.\n\n**BI-RADS 3 (probably benign)**: The chance of malignancy is 2% or less. Short-interval follow-up, usually at six months, avoids a biopsy for most women while keeping them safe. Stress that completing the follow-up schedule is what makes this approach safe.", "index_text": "Patient Communication in Breast Cancer Screening > Conveying Screening Results: **Normal results**: A normal mammogram is reassuring but does not rule out cancer. Mammography finds roughly 80–87% of cancers present, and fewer in dense breasts. Encourage continued screening at the recommended interval and prompt review of any new symptom.\n\n**Abnormal results**: Most abnormal screening mammograms are not cancer. About 10% of US screening mammograms lead to a recall for more imaging, and only about one in twenty recalled women has cancer. Explain that additional views or an ultrasound are a routine part of screening, not a diagnosis.\n\n**BI-RADS 3 (probably benign)**: The chance of malignancy is 2% or less. Short-interval follow-up, usually at six months, avoids a biopsy for most women while keeping them safe. Stress that completing the follow-up schedule is what makes this approach safe."}
+{"chunk_id": "patient_communication::conveying-screening-results::1", "source": "patient_communication", "title": "Patient Communication in Breast Cancer Screening", "section": "Conveying Screening Results", "text": "**BI-RADS 4 (suspicious)**: A biopsy will usually be recommended. Explain the level of suspicion (4A, 4B or 4C) and the approximate probability. In US benchmark data, about 29% of biopsies prompted by screening find cancer, so roughly seven in ten are benign. Describe the biopsy procedure, usually an image-guided core needle biopsy.\n\n**BI-RADS 5 (highly suggestive of malignancy)**: The probability of malignancy is 95% or more and a biopsy is required. Prepare the patient for a probable cancer diagnosis while explaining that treatment planning follows the biopsy result.", "index_text": "Patient Communication in Breast Cancer Screening > Conveying Screening Results: **BI-RADS 4 (suspicious)**: A biopsy will usually be recommended. Explain the level of suspicion (4A, 4B or 4C) and the approximate probability. In US benchmark data, about 29% of biopsies prompted by screening find cancer, so roughly seven in ten are benign. Describe the biopsy procedure, usually an image-guided core needle biopsy.\n\n**BI-RADS 5 (highly suggestive of malignancy)**: The probability of malignancy is 95% or more and a biopsy is required. Prepare the patient for a probable cancer diagnosis while explaining that treatment planning follows the biopsy result."}
+{"chunk_id": "patient_communication::psychological-impact::0", "source": "patient_communication", "title": "Patient Communication in Breast Cancer Screening", "section": "Psychological Impact", "text": "Screening results cause measurable distress. In a Danish cohort study, six months after a false-positive mammogram women reported changes in existential values and inner calmness as great as women diagnosed with breast cancer, and three years later they still reported more negative psychosocial consequences than women with a normal result. Clear, timely communication and a short wait for the follow-up test reduce this harm.", "index_text": "Patient Communication in Breast Cancer Screening > Psychological Impact: Screening results cause measurable distress. In a Danish cohort study, six months after a false-positive mammogram women reported changes in existential values and inner calmness as great as women diagnosed with breast cancer, and three years later they still reported more negative psychosocial consequences than women with a normal result. Clear, timely communication and a short wait for the follow-up test reduce this harm."}
+{"chunk_id": "patient_communication::best-practices-for-clinicians::0", "source": "patient_communication", "title": "Patient Communication in Breast Cancer Screening", "section": "Best Practices for Clinicians", "text": "Use plain language and avoid jargon. Give written material to supplement the conversation. Allow time for questions and acknowledge emotion. Use visual aids such as icon arrays to show probabilities. Frame numbers both ways when appropriate: \"about 85 in 100 findings like this are benign\" alongside \"about 15 in 100 are cancer\". Acknowledge uncertainty rather than offering false certainty, and refer to patient navigators or counselling when needed.", "index_text": "Patient Communication in Breast Cancer Screening > Best Practices for Clinicians: Use plain language and avoid jargon. Give written material to supplement the conversation. Allow time for questions and acknowledge emotion. Use visual aids such as icon arrays to show probabilities. Frame numbers both ways when appropriate: \"about 85 in 100 findings like this are benign\" alongside \"about 15 in 100 are cancer\". Acknowledge uncertainty rather than offering false certainty, and refer to patient navigators or counselling when needed."}
+{"chunk_id": "patient_communication::cultural-sensitivity::0", "source": "patient_communication", "title": "Patient Communication in Breast Cancer Screening", "section": "Cultural Sensitivity", "text": "Screening participation and the perception of risk vary across cultural backgrounds, and language barriers affect understanding of results. Beliefs about cancer can influence willingness to screen or to pursue treatment. Use culturally appropriate materials and professional interpreters rather than family members when needed.", "index_text": "Patient Communication in Breast Cancer Screening > Cultural Sensitivity: Screening participation and the perception of risk vary across cultural backgrounds, and language barriers affect understanding of results. Beliefs about cancer can influence willingness to screen or to pursue treatment. Use culturally appropriate materials and professional interpreters rather than family members when needed."}
+{"chunk_id": "patient_communication::common-misconceptions::0", "source": "patient_communication", "title": "Patient Communication in Breast Cancer Screening", "section": "Common Misconceptions", "text": "**\"Mammograms cause breast cancer.\"** A screening mammogram uses a small radiation dose, about 0.28 mSv (0.34 mSv for tomosynthesis), roughly a month of natural background radiation. The National Cancer Institute estimates that, in theory, annual mammograms between ages 40 and 80 could cause up to one breast cancer per 1,000 women. That theoretical risk is weighed against the reduction in breast cancer deaths that screening trials have shown, and it is not a reason to avoid recommended screening.", "index_text": "Patient Communication in Breast Cancer Screening > Common Misconceptions: **\"Mammograms cause breast cancer.\"** A screening mammogram uses a small radiation dose, about 0.28 mSv (0.34 mSv for tomosynthesis), roughly a month of natural background radiation. The National Cancer Institute estimates that, in theory, annual mammograms between ages 40 and 80 could cause up to one breast cancer per 1,000 women. That theoretical risk is weighed against the reduction in breast cancer deaths that screening trials have shown, and it is not a reason to avoid recommended screening."}
+{"chunk_id": "patient_communication::common-misconceptions::1", "source": "patient_communication", "title": "Patient Communication in Breast Cancer Screening", "section": "Common Misconceptions", "text": "**\"A biopsy spreads the cancer.\"** A core needle biopsy can displace tumour cells along the needle track. A systematic review found displaced cells in the surgical specimens of about 22% of patients, more often when surgery followed the biopsy quickly, but no study reported an increase in local recurrence, and the one study that examined survival found no worse survival after a needle biopsy. A biopsy is how a suspicious finding is diagnosed, and forgoing it carries the far larger risk of leaving a cancer untreated.\n\n**\"A normal mammogram rules out cancer.\"** Mammography finds roughly 80–87% of the cancers present, and fewer in dense breasts. A new lump or change in the breast needs assessment even after a recent normal screening mammogram.", "index_text": "Patient Communication in Breast Cancer Screening > Common Misconceptions: **\"A biopsy spreads the cancer.\"** A core needle biopsy can displace tumour cells along the needle track. A systematic review found displaced cells in the surgical specimens of about 22% of patients, more often when surgery followed the biopsy quickly, but no study reported an increase in local recurrence, and the one study that examined survival found no worse survival after a needle biopsy. A biopsy is how a suspicious finding is diagnosed, and forgoing it carries the far larger risk of leaving a cancer untreated.\n\n**\"A normal mammogram rules out cancer.\"** Mammography finds roughly 80–87% of the cancers present, and fewer in dense breasts. A new lump or change in the breast needs assessment even after a recent normal screening mammogram."}
+{"chunk_id": "patient_communication::common-misconceptions::2", "source": "patient_communication", "title": "Patient Communication in Breast Cancer Screening", "section": "Common Misconceptions", "text": "**\"A high AI estimate means I have cancer.\"** A model's probability describes how often masses with similar descriptors were malignant in its training data. It is not a diagnosis, and only a biopsy reviewed by a pathologist can confirm or exclude cancer.\n\n**\"A low AI estimate means it is benign.\"** A low estimate means that masses with similar descriptors were usually benign in the training data. It does not rule out cancer for any individual: the radiologist's assessment decides the next step, and only a biopsy can confirm or exclude cancer.\n\n**\"Antiperspirants or deodorants cause breast cancer.\"** The National Cancer Institute states that no scientific evidence links these products to the development of breast cancer, and the American Cancer Society lists antiperspirants among disproven or controversial risk factors.", "index_text": "Patient Communication in Breast Cancer Screening > Common Misconceptions: **\"A high AI estimate means I have cancer.\"** A model's probability describes how often masses with similar descriptors were malignant in its training data. It is not a diagnosis, and only a biopsy reviewed by a pathologist can confirm or exclude cancer.\n\n**\"A low AI estimate means it is benign.\"** A low estimate means that masses with similar descriptors were usually benign in the training data. It does not rule out cancer for any individual: the radiologist's assessment decides the next step, and only a biopsy can confirm or exclude cancer.\n\n**\"Antiperspirants or deodorants cause breast cancer.\"** The National Cancer Institute states that no scientific evidence links these products to the development of breast cancer, and the American Cancer Society lists antiperspirants among disproven or controversial risk factors."}
+{"chunk_id": "patient_communication::common-misconceptions::3", "source": "patient_communication", "title": "Patient Communication in Breast Cancer Screening", "section": "Common Misconceptions", "text": "**\"Wearing a bra, including an underwire bra, causes breast cancer.\"** The American Cancer Society states that there are no scientific or clinical data showing that wearing a bra changes the risk of breast cancer.\n\n**\"No one in my family had breast cancer, so this cannot be cancer.\"** More than 85% of women diagnosed with breast cancer have no family history of the disease, according to the American Cancer Society, and only up to about 10% of breast cancers are thought to be hereditary. A missing family history does not rule cancer out.\n\n**\"Dense breasts are a disease.\"** The National Cancer Institute states that dense breasts are not an abnormal breast condition or a disease. They are common (found in nearly half of women aged 40 and over who have mammograms), but they do raise breast cancer risk and make mammograms harder to read.", "index_text": "Patient Communication in Breast Cancer Screening > Common Misconceptions: **\"Wearing a bra, including an underwire bra, causes breast cancer.\"** The American Cancer Society states that there are no scientific or clinical data showing that wearing a bra changes the risk of breast cancer.\n\n**\"No one in my family had breast cancer, so this cannot be cancer.\"** More than 85% of women diagnosed with breast cancer have no family history of the disease, according to the American Cancer Society, and only up to about 10% of breast cancers are thought to be hereditary. A missing family history does not rule cancer out.\n\n**\"Dense breasts are a disease.\"** The National Cancer Institute states that dense breasts are not an abnormal breast condition or a disease. They are common (found in nearly half of women aged 40 and over who have mammograms), but they do raise breast cancer risk and make mammograms harder to read."}
+{"chunk_id": "patient_communication::common-misconceptions::4", "source": "patient_communication", "title": "Patient Communication in Breast Cancer Screening", "section": "Common Misconceptions", "text": "**\"If a lump does not hurt, it cannot be cancer.\"** A painless, hard, fixed mass with irregular edges is more likely to be cancer, but breast cancer can also be soft, round, tender or even painful, according to the American Cancer Society. Pain neither rules cancer in nor out.", "index_text": "Patient Communication in Breast Cancer Screening > Common Misconceptions: **\"If a lump does not hurt, it cannot be cancer.\"** A painless, hard, fixed mass with irregular edges is more likely to be cancer, but breast cancer can also be soft, round, tender or even painful, according to the American Cancer Society. Pain neither rules cancer in nor out."}
+{"chunk_id": "patient_communication::decision-making-frameworks::0", "source": "patient_communication", "title": "Patient Communication in Breast Cancer Screening", "section": "Decision-Making Frameworks", "text": "In shared decision-making the clinician presents the evidence-based options and the patient chooses according to her values. It matters most where guidelines differ or the options are close: when to start screening (40 or 45) and how often (annually or every two years), whether to add supplemental imaging for dense breasts, whether to follow up or biopsy a probably benign finding, and the choice between breast-conserving surgery and mastectomy for early cancers.", "index_text": "Patient Communication in Breast Cancer Screening > Decision-Making Frameworks: In shared decision-making the clinician presents the evidence-based options and the patient chooses according to her values. It matters most where guidelines differ or the options are close: when to start screening (40 or 45) and how often (annually or every two years), whether to add supplemental imaging for dense breasts, whether to follow up or biopsy a probably benign finding, and the choice between breast-conserving surgery and mastectomy for early cancers."}
+{"chunk_id": "risk_factors_epidemiology::incidence-and-mortality::0", "source": "risk_factors_epidemiology", "title": "Breast Cancer Risk Factors and Epidemiology", "section": "Incidence and Mortality", "text": "Breast cancer is the most frequently diagnosed cancer in women worldwide. GLOBOCAN 2020 estimated about 2.3 million new cases and 685,000 deaths in 2020, roughly one in four cancers diagnosed in women. In the United States, SEER estimates about 322,000 new cases of female breast cancer and 42,000 deaths in 2026, and about 13% of women will be diagnosed with breast cancer during their lifetime.\n\nSurvival depends strongly on the stage at diagnosis. In US SEER data (diagnoses 2016–2022), five-year relative survival was 91.9% overall: about 100% for localized disease (confined to the breast), 87.5% for regional disease (spread to nearby lymph nodes) and 33.8% for distant metastatic disease. Relative survival compares patients with similar women without breast cancer, so a figure near 100% means almost no excess deaths within five years.", "index_text": "Breast Cancer Risk Factors and Epidemiology > Incidence and Mortality: Breast cancer is the most frequently diagnosed cancer in women worldwide. GLOBOCAN 2020 estimated about 2.3 million new cases and 685,000 deaths in 2020, roughly one in four cancers diagnosed in women. In the United States, SEER estimates about 322,000 new cases of female breast cancer and 42,000 deaths in 2026, and about 13% of women will be diagnosed with breast cancer during their lifetime.\n\nSurvival depends strongly on the stage at diagnosis. In US SEER data (diagnoses 2016–2022), five-year relative survival was 91.9% overall: about 100% for localized disease (confined to the breast), 87.5% for regional disease (spread to nearby lymph nodes) and 33.8% for distant metastatic disease. Relative survival compares patients with similar women without breast cancer, so a figure near 100% means almost no excess deaths within five years."}
+{"chunk_id": "risk_factors_epidemiology::age-specific-incidence::0", "source": "risk_factors_epidemiology", "title": "Breast Cancer Risk Factors and Epidemiology", "section": "Age-Specific Incidence", "text": "Breast cancer risk rises with age. In US SEER data (2019–2023), the share of new female breast cancers by age at diagnosis was: under 20, 0%; 20–34, 2.0%; 35–44, 8.6%; 45–54, 17.7%; 55–64, 24.3%; 65–74, 27.5%; 75–84, 15.1%; 85 and over, 4.9%. Breast cancer is most frequently diagnosed between 65 and 74, and the median age at diagnosis is 64. About one case in ten occurs before age 45.", "index_text": "Breast Cancer Risk Factors and Epidemiology > Age-Specific Incidence: Breast cancer risk rises with age. In US SEER data (2019–2023), the share of new female breast cancers by age at diagnosis was: under 20, 0%; 20–34, 2.0%; 35–44, 8.6%; 45–54, 17.7%; 55–64, 24.3%; 65–74, 27.5%; 75–84, 15.1%; 85 and over, 4.9%. Breast cancer is most frequently diagnosed between 65 and 74, and the median age at diagnosis is 64. About one case in ten occurs before age 45."}
+{"chunk_id": "risk_factors_epidemiology::non-modifiable-risk-factors::0", "source": "risk_factors_epidemiology", "title": "Breast Cancer Risk Factors and Epidemiology", "section": "Non-Modifiable Risk Factors", "text": "**Age**: After female sex, age is the strongest risk factor. Most breast cancers are diagnosed after age 50.\n\n**Sex**: Female sex is the main risk factor. Male breast cancer accounts for under 1% of cases.\n\n**Inherited mutations**: Women with a BRCA1 mutation have a 55–72% risk of breast cancer by age 70–80, and BRCA2 carriers 45–69%, depending on the study. TP53 mutations (Li-Fraumeni syndrome) carry a near-certain lifetime risk of some cancer, with breast cancer in about half of female carriers by age 70. PALB2 mutations raise breast cancer risk roughly five-fold, and ATM or CHEK2 mutations roughly two-fold.\n\n**Family history**: One first-degree relative (mother, sister or daughter) with breast cancer raises risk about 1.8-fold, and two first-degree relatives about 2.9-fold. A relative diagnosed at a young age raises risk more than one diagnosed late in life.", "index_text": "Breast Cancer Risk Factors and Epidemiology > Non-Modifiable Risk Factors: **Age**: After female sex, age is the strongest risk factor. Most breast cancers are diagnosed after age 50.\n\n**Sex**: Female sex is the main risk factor. Male breast cancer accounts for under 1% of cases.\n\n**Inherited mutations**: Women with a BRCA1 mutation have a 55–72% risk of breast cancer by age 70–80, and BRCA2 carriers 45–69%, depending on the study. TP53 mutations (Li-Fraumeni syndrome) carry a near-certain lifetime risk of some cancer, with breast cancer in about half of female carriers by age 70. PALB2 mutations raise breast cancer risk roughly five-fold, and ATM or CHEK2 mutations roughly two-fold.\n\n**Family history**: One first-degree relative (mother, sister or daughter) with breast cancer raises risk about 1.8-fold, and two first-degree relatives about 2.9-fold. A relative diagnosed at a young age raises risk more than one diagnosed late in life."}
+{"chunk_id": "risk_factors_epidemiology::non-modifiable-risk-factors::1", "source": "risk_factors_epidemiology", "title": "Breast Cancer Risk Factors and Epidemiology", "section": "Non-Modifiable Risk Factors", "text": "**Personal history**: After breast cancer in one breast, the risk of a new cancer in the other breast is about 0.4% per year. Atypical ductal hyperplasia raises risk about four- to five-fold, and lobular carcinoma in situ about seven- to twelve-fold, relative to women without these findings.\n\n**Reproductive factors**: Early menarche (first period before 12), late menopause (after 55), never having given birth, and a first pregnancy late in life each raise risk modestly, mainly through longer lifetime exposure to oestrogen.", "index_text": "Breast Cancer Risk Factors and Epidemiology > Non-Modifiable Risk Factors: **Personal history**: After breast cancer in one breast, the risk of a new cancer in the other breast is about 0.4% per year. Atypical ductal hyperplasia raises risk about four- to five-fold, and lobular carcinoma in situ about seven- to twelve-fold, relative to women without these findings.\n\n**Reproductive factors**: Early menarche (first period before 12), late menopause (after 55), never having given birth, and a first pregnancy late in life each raise risk modestly, mainly through longer lifetime exposure to oestrogen."}
+{"chunk_id": "risk_factors_epidemiology::modifiable-risk-factors::0", "source": "risk_factors_epidemiology", "title": "Breast Cancer Risk Factors and Epidemiology", "section": "Modifiable Risk Factors", "text": "**Physical inactivity**: In pooled studies, the most physically active women had a 12–21% lower risk of breast cancer than the least active, for both premenopausal and postmenopausal women.\n\n**Obesity after menopause**: Higher body mass index raises postmenopausal breast cancer risk by roughly 12% for every 5 kg/m², because fat tissue produces oestrogen through the enzyme aromatase.\n\n**Alcohol**: Risk rises with the amount consumed, by about 7% for every 10 g of alcohol (roughly one standard drink) per day, and even light drinking raises risk slightly.\n\n**Menopausal hormone therapy**: Combined oestrogen–progestogen therapy raises breast cancer risk from the first years of use (about 1.6-fold during years 1–4 of current use), and some excess risk persists for more than ten years after stopping.", "index_text": "Breast Cancer Risk Factors and Epidemiology > Modifiable Risk Factors: **Physical inactivity**: In pooled studies, the most physically active women had a 12–21% lower risk of breast cancer than the least active, for both premenopausal and postmenopausal women.\n\n**Obesity after menopause**: Higher body mass index raises postmenopausal breast cancer risk by roughly 12% for every 5 kg/m², because fat tissue produces oestrogen through the enzyme aromatase.\n\n**Alcohol**: Risk rises with the amount consumed, by about 7% for every 10 g of alcohol (roughly one standard drink) per day, and even light drinking raises risk slightly.\n\n**Menopausal hormone therapy**: Combined oestrogen–progestogen therapy raises breast cancer risk from the first years of use (about 1.6-fold during years 1–4 of current use), and some excess risk persists for more than ten years after stopping."}
+{"chunk_id": "risk_factors_epidemiology::modifiable-risk-factors::1", "source": "risk_factors_epidemiology", "title": "Breast Cancer Risk Factors and Epidemiology", "section": "Modifiable Risk Factors", "text": "**Smoking**: Smoking is associated with a modest increase in risk, about 10%, with the strongest associations for women who started smoking early or before a first pregnancy.", "index_text": "Breast Cancer Risk Factors and Epidemiology > Modifiable Risk Factors: **Smoking**: Smoking is associated with a modest increase in risk, about 10%, with the strongest associations for women who started smoking early or before a first pregnancy."}
+{"chunk_id": "risk_factors_epidemiology::breast-density::0", "source": "risk_factors_epidemiology", "title": "Breast Cancer Risk Factors and Epidemiology", "section": "Breast Density", "text": "The ACR BI-RADS density categories are: A, almost entirely fatty (about 10% of women); B, scattered areas of fibroglandular density (about 40%); C, heterogeneously dense (about 40%); and D, extremely dense (about 10%). Nearly half of women aged 40 and over who have mammograms have dense breasts (category C or D).\n\nDensity matters twice. Dense tissue and many cancers both appear white on a mammogram, so density lowers mammographic sensitivity (masking). Dense breasts are also an independent risk factor: compared with category B, breast cancer risk is roughly 1.4–1.6 times higher in category C and 1.6–1.8 times higher in category D.", "index_text": "Breast Cancer Risk Factors and Epidemiology > Breast Density: The ACR BI-RADS density categories are: A, almost entirely fatty (about 10% of women); B, scattered areas of fibroglandular density (about 40%); C, heterogeneously dense (about 40%); and D, extremely dense (about 10%). Nearly half of women aged 40 and over who have mammograms have dense breasts (category C or D).\n\nDensity matters twice. Dense tissue and many cancers both appear white on a mammogram, so density lowers mammographic sensitivity (masking). Dense breasts are also an independent risk factor: compared with category B, breast cancer risk is roughly 1.4–1.6 times higher in category C and 1.6–1.8 times higher in category D."}
+{"chunk_id": "risk_factors_epidemiology::lifetime-risk-categories::0", "source": "risk_factors_epidemiology", "title": "Breast Cancer Risk Factors and Epidemiology", "section": "Lifetime Risk Categories", "text": "The average lifetime risk for a woman in the United States is about 13%. Guidelines use lifetime-risk thresholds to decide who should receive supplemental MRI screening: the American Cancer Society recommends annual MRI with mammography for women with a lifetime risk of about 20–25% or greater, recommends against MRI screening when lifetime risk is below 15%, and finds the evidence insufficient between 15% and 20%. NCCN uses a residual lifetime risk of 20% or greater, calculated with a model based on comprehensive family history, as the threshold for its increased-risk screening schedule.", "index_text": "Breast Cancer Risk Factors and Epidemiology > Lifetime Risk Categories: The average lifetime risk for a woman in the United States is about 13%. Guidelines use lifetime-risk thresholds to decide who should receive supplemental MRI screening: the American Cancer Society recommends annual MRI with mammography for women with a lifetime risk of about 20–25% or greater, recommends against MRI screening when lifetime risk is below 15%, and finds the evidence insufficient between 15% and 20%. NCCN uses a residual lifetime risk of 20% or greater, calculated with a model based on comprehensive family history, as the threshold for its increased-risk screening schedule."}
+{"chunk_id": "safety_disclaimer::what-this-tool-is::0", "source": "safety_disclaimer", "title": "Safety Disclaimer — BreastCare AI Scope, Limitations, and Patient Guidance", "section": "What This Tool Is", "text": "BreastCare AI is a research and educational prototype. It estimates the probability that a breast mass is malignant from four values: the patient's age and three BI-RADS descriptors of the mass (shape, margin and density). The estimate comes from a statistical model trained on the UCI Mammographic Mass dataset, a public collection of 961 masses (831 with complete descriptors) from one German radiology institute between 2003 and 2006. The model was chosen by a fixed rule from a logistic regression and two LightGBM candidates; the app shows which model is currently served.", "index_text": "Safety Disclaimer — BreastCare AI Scope, Limitations, and Patient Guidance > What This Tool Is: BreastCare AI is a research and educational prototype. It estimates the probability that a breast mass is malignant from four values: the patient's age and three BI-RADS descriptors of the mass (shape, margin and density). The estimate comes from a statistical model trained on the UCI Mammographic Mass dataset, a public collection of 961 masses (831 with complete descriptors) from one German radiology institute between 2003 and 2006. The model was chosen by a fixed rule from a logistic regression and two LightGBM candidates; the app shows which model is currently served."}
+{"chunk_id": "safety_disclaimer::what-the-outputs-mean::0", "source": "safety_disclaimer", "title": "Safety Disclaimer — BreastCare AI Scope, Limitations, and Patient Guidance", "section": "What the Outputs Mean", "text": "The tool shows a calibrated probability, which estimates how often masses with the same four descriptors were malignant in the study population. It also shows a 90% prediction set: {Benign}, {Malignant}, or both. A set containing both labels means the model cannot separate the two outcomes for these inputs. The feature contributions (SHAP values) show which of the four inputs pushed the estimate up or down. None of these outputs is a diagnosis.", "index_text": "Safety Disclaimer — BreastCare AI Scope, Limitations, and Patient Guidance > What the Outputs Mean: The tool shows a calibrated probability, which estimates how often masses with the same four descriptors were malignant in the study population. It also shows a 90% prediction set: {Benign}, {Malignant}, or both. A set containing both labels means the model cannot separate the two outcomes for these inputs. The feature contributions (SHAP values) show which of the four inputs pushed the estimate up or down. None of these outputs is a diagnosis."}
+{"chunk_id": "safety_disclaimer::what-this-tool-is-not::0", "source": "safety_disclaimer", "title": "Safety Disclaimer — BreastCare AI Scope, Limitations, and Patient Guidance", "section": "What This Tool Is Not", "text": "This tool is not a medical device. It has not been cleared or approved by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) or any other regulator, and it has not been validated in a clinical trial. It does not diagnose cancer, does not replace a radiologist's reading or a biopsy, and does not recommend treatment.\n\nThe image-analysis feature is a separate, experimental model whose accuracy has not been measured. It is not part of the risk estimate and may be switched off on a given server.", "index_text": "Safety Disclaimer — BreastCare AI Scope, Limitations, and Patient Guidance > What This Tool Is Not: This tool is not a medical device. It has not been cleared or approved by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) or any other regulator, and it has not been validated in a clinical trial. It does not diagnose cancer, does not replace a radiologist's reading or a biopsy, and does not recommend treatment.\n\nThe image-analysis feature is a separate, experimental model whose accuracy has not been measured. It is not part of the risk estimate and may be switched off on a given server."}
+{"chunk_id": "safety_disclaimer::clinical-limitations::0", "source": "safety_disclaimer", "title": "Safety Disclaimer — BreastCare AI Scope, Limitations, and Patient Guidance", "section": "Clinical Limitations", "text": "The model sees only four coarse descriptors, which radiologists assign by eye, so it inherits differences between readers. It was trained and tested on data from one institution and may not generalise to other screening programmes, equipment or populations. Its test set was small, so its accuracy figures have wide uncertainty intervals, and its performance differs between age groups. The model card and evaluation report in the project's documentation give the measured figures.", "index_text": "Safety Disclaimer — BreastCare AI Scope, Limitations, and Patient Guidance > Clinical Limitations: The model sees only four coarse descriptors, which radiologists assign by eye, so it inherits differences between readers. It was trained and tested on data from one institution and may not generalise to other screening programmes, equipment or populations. Its test set was small, so its accuracy figures have wide uncertainty intervals, and its performance differs between age groups. The model card and evaluation report in the project's documentation give the measured figures."}
+{"chunk_id": "safety_disclaimer::high-risk-estimate-notice::0", "source": "safety_disclaimer", "title": "Safety Disclaimer — BreastCare AI Scope, Limitations, and Patient Guidance", "section": "High-Risk Estimate Notice", "text": "When the estimated malignancy probability is 65% or higher, the tool displays an enhanced notice. A high estimate does not mean a diagnosis of cancer. It means that masses with the same descriptors were often malignant in the training data. Only a biopsy, examined by a pathologist, can confirm or exclude malignancy.", "index_text": "Safety Disclaimer — BreastCare AI Scope, Limitations, and Patient Guidance > High-Risk Estimate Notice: When the estimated malignancy probability is 65% or higher, the tool displays an enhanced notice. A high estimate does not mean a diagnosis of cancer. It means that masses with the same descriptors were often malignant in the training data. Only a biopsy, examined by a pathologist, can confirm or exclude malignancy."}
+{"chunk_id": "safety_disclaimer::what-you-should-do::0", "source": "safety_disclaimer", "title": "Safety Disclaimer — BreastCare AI Scope, Limitations, and Patient Guidance", "section": "What You Should Do", "text": "If you have received an abnormal mammography result, or have concerns about a breast finding, speak to a qualified radiologist or physician. Do not use this tool's output to make, delay or skip any clinical decision, including a recommended biopsy or follow-up appointment.\n\nIf you are feeling overwhelmed or having thoughts of harming yourself, contact your local emergency number or a crisis line. In the US, call or text 988; in the UK and Ireland, call Samaritans on 116 123; elsewhere, findahelpline.com lists free helplines.", "index_text": "Safety Disclaimer — BreastCare AI Scope, Limitations, and Patient Guidance > What You Should Do: If you have received an abnormal mammography result, or have concerns about a breast finding, speak to a qualified radiologist or physician. Do not use this tool's output to make, delay or skip any clinical decision, including a recommended biopsy or follow-up appointment.\n\nIf you are feeling overwhelmed or having thoughts of harming yourself, contact your local emergency number or a crisis line. In the US, call or text 988; in the UK and Ireland, call Samaritans on 116 123; elsewhere, findahelpline.com lists free helplines."}
+{"chunk_id": "safety_disclaimer::privacy::0", "source": "safety_disclaimer", "title": "Safety Disclaimer — BreastCare AI Scope, Limitations, and Patient Guidance", "section": "Privacy", "text": "The tool does not ask for your name or contact details. Questions typed into the chat are sent to Google's Gemini API to generate an answer. The server can keep an audit log of assessments (the four values entered and the estimate) when a session is started through the API; the web interface does not start one. Please do not type identifying personal information into the chat.", "index_text": "Safety Disclaimer — BreastCare AI Scope, Limitations, and Patient Guidance > Privacy: The tool does not ask for your name or contact details. Questions typed into the chat are sent to Google's Gemini API to generate an answer. The server can keep an audit log of assessments (the four values entered and the estimate) when a session is started through the API; the web interface does not start one. Please do not type identifying personal information into the chat."}
+{"chunk_id": "safety_disclaimer::contact-and-research-context::0", "source": "safety_disclaimer", "title": "Safety Disclaimer — BreastCare AI Scope, Limitations, and Patient Guidance", "section": "Contact and Research Context", "text": "BreastCare AI was developed as an academic research prototype demonstrating how gradient-boosted and linear risk models, calibration, conformal prediction, SHAP explanations, retrieval-augmented generation with Google Gemini, and safety guardrails can be combined and evaluated for educational purposes in the context of breast cancer screening research.", "index_text": "Safety Disclaimer — BreastCare AI Scope, Limitations, and Patient Guidance > Contact and Research Context: BreastCare AI was developed as an academic research prototype demonstrating how gradient-boosted and linear risk models, calibration, conformal prediction, SHAP explanations, retrieval-augmented generation with Google Gemini, and safety guardrails can be combined and evaluated for educational purposes in the context of breast cancer screening research."}
+{"chunk_id": "treatment_pathways::overview::0", "source": "treatment_pathways", "title": "Breast Cancer Treatment Pathways", "section": "Overview", "text": "Breast cancer treatment is multimodal and individualised. The pathway depends on the stage, the tumour's biology (hormone-receptor and HER2 status, grade, and sometimes a genomic test), and the patient's age, other illnesses and preferences. Decisions are made by a multidisciplinary team; this document describes the main building blocks, not a recommendation for any individual.", "index_text": "Breast Cancer Treatment Pathways > Overview: Breast cancer treatment is multimodal and individualised. The pathway depends on the stage, the tumour's biology (hormone-receptor and HER2 status, grade, and sometimes a genomic test), and the patient's age, other illnesses and preferences. Decisions are made by a multidisciplinary team; this document describes the main building blocks, not a recommendation for any individual."}
+{"chunk_id": "treatment_pathways::treatment-modalities-surgery::0", "source": "treatment_pathways", "title": "Breast Cancer Treatment Pathways", "section": "Treatment Modalities > Surgery", "text": "**Breast-conserving surgery (lumpectomy)**: Removes the tumour with a rim of normal tissue and is usually followed by radiation therapy. For appropriately selected early-stage cancers it gives the same survival as mastectomy.\n\n**Mastectomy**: Removes the whole breast. It is chosen when the tumour is large relative to the breast, when there are several tumours in different parts of the breast, after previous breast radiation, or by patient preference. Variants include skin-sparing and nipple-sparing mastectomy with reconstruction.\n\n**Sentinel lymph node biopsy**: Removes the first lymph node(s) draining the breast to check for spread. When the sentinel nodes are clear, a full axillary dissection is avoided, which lowers the risk of lymphoedema.", "index_text": "Breast Cancer Treatment Pathways > Treatment Modalities > Surgery: **Breast-conserving surgery (lumpectomy)**: Removes the tumour with a rim of normal tissue and is usually followed by radiation therapy. For appropriately selected early-stage cancers it gives the same survival as mastectomy.\n\n**Mastectomy**: Removes the whole breast. It is chosen when the tumour is large relative to the breast, when there are several tumours in different parts of the breast, after previous breast radiation, or by patient preference. Variants include skin-sparing and nipple-sparing mastectomy with reconstruction.\n\n**Sentinel lymph node biopsy**: Removes the first lymph node(s) draining the breast to check for spread. When the sentinel nodes are clear, a full axillary dissection is avoided, which lowers the risk of lymphoedema."}
+{"chunk_id": "treatment_pathways::treatment-modalities-radiation-therapy::0", "source": "treatment_pathways", "title": "Breast Cancer Treatment Pathways", "section": "Treatment Modalities > Radiation Therapy", "text": "**Whole-breast radiation**: Standard after breast-conserving surgery. In the pooled randomised trials, radiation roughly halved the ten-year risk of recurrence and reduced breast cancer deaths by about one-sixth. Hypofractionated schedules of three to four weeks are standard, and a one-week, five-fraction schedule was non-inferior in a large UK trial.\n\n**Partial-breast irradiation**: Treats only the area around the tumour bed over a shorter course, for selected low-risk patients.\n\n**Post-mastectomy radiation**: Recommended when four or more lymph nodes are involved, the tumour is larger than 5 cm, or the margins are positive, and considered when one to three nodes are involved. It reduces chest-wall recurrence and, in node-positive disease, breast cancer deaths.", "index_text": "Breast Cancer Treatment Pathways > Treatment Modalities > Radiation Therapy: **Whole-breast radiation**: Standard after breast-conserving surgery. In the pooled randomised trials, radiation roughly halved the ten-year risk of recurrence and reduced breast cancer deaths by about one-sixth. Hypofractionated schedules of three to four weeks are standard, and a one-week, five-fraction schedule was non-inferior in a large UK trial.\n\n**Partial-breast irradiation**: Treats only the area around the tumour bed over a shorter course, for selected low-risk patients.\n\n**Post-mastectomy radiation**: Recommended when four or more lymph nodes are involved, the tumour is larger than 5 cm, or the margins are positive, and considered when one to three nodes are involved. It reduces chest-wall recurrence and, in node-positive disease, breast cancer deaths."}
+{"chunk_id": "treatment_pathways::treatment-modalities-systemic-therapy::0", "source": "treatment_pathways", "title": "Breast Cancer Treatment Pathways", "section": "Treatment Modalities > Systemic Therapy", "text": "**Chemotherapy**: Given before surgery (neoadjuvant) or after it (adjuvant). Common regimens are AC-T (doxorubicin and cyclophosphamide followed by paclitaxel) and TC (docetaxel and cyclophosphamide). Neoadjuvant chemotherapy can shrink a tumour enough to allow breast conservation, and is standard for stage II–III triple-negative and HER2-positive cancers.\n\n**Endocrine (hormonal) therapy**: For hormone receptor-positive cancers, which make up roughly 70–80% of breast cancers. Tamoxifen can be used before or after the menopause; aromatase inhibitors (anastrozole, letrozole, exemestane) are used after the menopause, or before it together with ovarian suppression. Treatment lasts at least five years and up to ten for higher-risk disease.", "index_text": "Breast Cancer Treatment Pathways > Treatment Modalities > Systemic Therapy: **Chemotherapy**: Given before surgery (neoadjuvant) or after it (adjuvant). Common regimens are AC-T (doxorubicin and cyclophosphamide followed by paclitaxel) and TC (docetaxel and cyclophosphamide). Neoadjuvant chemotherapy can shrink a tumour enough to allow breast conservation, and is standard for stage II–III triple-negative and HER2-positive cancers.\n\n**Endocrine (hormonal) therapy**: For hormone receptor-positive cancers, which make up roughly 70–80% of breast cancers. Tamoxifen can be used before or after the menopause; aromatase inhibitors (anastrozole, letrozole, exemestane) are used after the menopause, or before it together with ovarian suppression. Treatment lasts at least five years and up to ten for higher-risk disease."}
+{"chunk_id": "treatment_pathways::treatment-modalities-systemic-therapy::1", "source": "treatment_pathways", "title": "Breast Cancer Treatment Pathways", "section": "Treatment Modalities > Systemic Therapy", "text": "**Targeted therapy**: HER2-positive cancers (about 15–20%) are treated with trastuzumab, often with pertuzumab, and with the antibody–drug conjugates T-DM1 and trastuzumab deruxtecan. CDK4/6 inhibitors (palbociclib, ribociclib, abemaciclib) are standard with endocrine therapy for advanced hormone receptor-positive, HER2-negative disease, and abemaciclib and ribociclib are also used for high-risk early disease. PARP inhibitors (olaparib, talazoparib) are used for HER2-negative cancers in carriers of a germline BRCA mutation, including olaparib after surgery for high-risk early cancers.\n\n**Immunotherapy**: Pembrolizumab with chemotherapy is used for early-stage triple-negative breast cancer (before and after surgery, regardless of PD-L1 status) and for PD-L1-positive metastatic triple-negative disease.", "index_text": "Breast Cancer Treatment Pathways > Treatment Modalities > Systemic Therapy: **Targeted therapy**: HER2-positive cancers (about 15–20%) are treated with trastuzumab, often with pertuzumab, and with the antibody–drug conjugates T-DM1 and trastuzumab deruxtecan. CDK4/6 inhibitors (palbociclib, ribociclib, abemaciclib) are standard with endocrine therapy for advanced hormone receptor-positive, HER2-negative disease, and abemaciclib and ribociclib are also used for high-risk early disease. PARP inhibitors (olaparib, talazoparib) are used for HER2-negative cancers in carriers of a germline BRCA mutation, including olaparib after surgery for high-risk early cancers.\n\n**Immunotherapy**: Pembrolizumab with chemotherapy is used for early-stage triple-negative breast cancer (before and after surgery, regardless of PD-L1 status) and for PD-L1-positive metastatic triple-negative disease."}
+{"chunk_id": "treatment_pathways::treatment-by-stage::0", "source": "treatment_pathways", "title": "Breast Cancer Treatment Pathways", "section": "Treatment by Stage", "text": "**Stage 0 (ductal carcinoma in situ)**: Breast-conserving surgery with radiation, or mastectomy. Endocrine therapy may be offered for oestrogen receptor-positive DCIS to lower the risk of a new cancer. Chemotherapy is not used.\n\n**Stage I**: Breast-conserving surgery with radiation (preferred) or mastectomy, with sentinel node biopsy. Systemic therapy depends on receptor status and, for hormone receptor-positive HER2-negative cancers, often on a genomic test.\n\n**Stage II**: Surgery is chosen according to tumour and breast size, with axillary staging. Neoadjuvant therapy is often used, both to allow breast conservation and, in triple-negative and HER2-positive disease, to tailor treatment after surgery to the response.\n\n**Stage III (locally advanced)**: Neoadjuvant systemic therapy to shrink the tumour, then surgery and radiation, with further systemic treatment as indicated.", "index_text": "Breast Cancer Treatment Pathways > Treatment by Stage: **Stage 0 (ductal carcinoma in situ)**: Breast-conserving surgery with radiation, or mastectomy. Endocrine therapy may be offered for oestrogen receptor-positive DCIS to lower the risk of a new cancer. Chemotherapy is not used.\n\n**Stage I**: Breast-conserving surgery with radiation (preferred) or mastectomy, with sentinel node biopsy. Systemic therapy depends on receptor status and, for hormone receptor-positive HER2-negative cancers, often on a genomic test.\n\n**Stage II**: Surgery is chosen according to tumour and breast size, with axillary staging. Neoadjuvant therapy is often used, both to allow breast conservation and, in triple-negative and HER2-positive disease, to tailor treatment after surgery to the response.\n\n**Stage III (locally advanced)**: Neoadjuvant systemic therapy to shrink the tumour, then surgery and radiation, with further systemic treatment as indicated."}
+{"chunk_id": "treatment_pathways::treatment-by-stage::1", "source": "treatment_pathways", "title": "Breast Cancer Treatment Pathways", "section": "Treatment by Stage", "text": "**Stage IV (metastatic)**: The goal is to control the disease and preserve quality of life for as long as possible; cure is rare. Treatment is systemic (endocrine, targeted, chemotherapy or immunotherapy, according to tumour biology), with radiation or surgery for specific symptoms.", "index_text": "Breast Cancer Treatment Pathways > Treatment by Stage: **Stage IV (metastatic)**: The goal is to control the disease and preserve quality of life for as long as possible; cure is rare. Treatment is systemic (endocrine, targeted, chemotherapy or immunotherapy, according to tumour biology), with radiation or surgery for specific symptoms."}
+{"chunk_id": "treatment_pathways::genomic-testing::0", "source": "treatment_pathways", "title": "Breast Cancer Treatment Pathways", "section": "Genomic Testing", "text": "**Oncotype DX**: A 21-gene recurrence score for hormone receptor-positive, HER2-negative early breast cancer. In the TAILORx trial, women with scores of 0–10 did well with endocrine therapy alone, and for scores of 11–25 endocrine therapy alone was not inferior to adding chemotherapy overall, although women aged 50 or younger with scores of 16–25 gained some benefit from chemotherapy. Scores of 26 or above indicate a likely chemotherapy benefit.\n\n**MammaPrint**: A 70-gene signature classifying tumours as low or high genomic risk. In the MINDACT trial, women at high clinical risk but low genomic risk had good outcomes without chemotherapy.", "index_text": "Breast Cancer Treatment Pathways > Genomic Testing: **Oncotype DX**: A 21-gene recurrence score for hormone receptor-positive, HER2-negative early breast cancer. In the TAILORx trial, women with scores of 0–10 did well with endocrine therapy alone, and for scores of 11–25 endocrine therapy alone was not inferior to adding chemotherapy overall, although women aged 50 or younger with scores of 16–25 gained some benefit from chemotherapy. Scores of 26 or above indicate a likely chemotherapy benefit.\n\n**MammaPrint**: A 70-gene signature classifying tumours as low or high genomic risk. In the MINDACT trial, women at high clinical risk but low genomic risk had good outcomes without chemotherapy."}
+{"chunk_id": "who_screening_protocols::global-context::0", "source": "who_screening_protocols", "title": "WHO Breast Cancer Screening and Early Diagnosis", "section": "Global Context", "text": "Breast cancer is the most common cancer in women and one of the two most commonly diagnosed cancers overall, with about 2.3 million new cases and 685,000 deaths in 2020 (GLOBOCAN 2020). Most deaths occur in low- and middle-income countries, where cancers are more often diagnosed late. Mammographic screening is the only screening method shown in randomised trials to reduce breast cancer mortality.", "index_text": "WHO Breast Cancer Screening and Early Diagnosis > Global Context: Breast cancer is the most common cancer in women and one of the two most commonly diagnosed cancers overall, with about 2.3 million new cases and 685,000 deaths in 2020 (GLOBOCAN 2020). Most deaths occur in low- and middle-income countries, where cancers are more often diagnosed late. Mammographic screening is the only screening method shown in randomised trials to reduce breast cancer mortality."}
+{"chunk_id": "who_screening_protocols::who-position-on-mammographic-screening::0", "source": "who_screening_protocols", "title": "WHO Breast Cancer Screening and Early Diagnosis", "section": "WHO Position on Mammographic Screening", "text": "The WHO position paper on mammography screening (2014) recommends organised, population-based mammography screening only in well-resourced settings where the health system can guarantee quality assurance, follow-up of abnormal results and treatment. It recommends biennial screening of women aged 50–69 in such settings. Screening women aged 40–49 or 70–75 is suggested only within rigorous research or in programmes that can show their net benefit. Screening without the capacity to diagnose and treat the cancers found causes harm, through false positives, overdiagnosis and overtreatment, without the benefit.", "index_text": "WHO Breast Cancer Screening and Early Diagnosis > WHO Position on Mammographic Screening: The WHO position paper on mammography screening (2014) recommends organised, population-based mammography screening only in well-resourced settings where the health system can guarantee quality assurance, follow-up of abnormal results and treatment. It recommends biennial screening of women aged 50–69 in such settings. Screening women aged 40–49 or 70–75 is suggested only within rigorous research or in programmes that can show their net benefit. Screening without the capacity to diagnose and treat the cancers found causes harm, through false positives, overdiagnosis and overtreatment, without the benefit."}
+{"chunk_id": "who_screening_protocols::recommendations-by-resource-setting::0", "source": "who_screening_protocols", "title": "WHO Breast Cancer Screening and Early Diagnosis", "section": "Recommendations by Resource Setting", "text": "**Well-resourced settings**: Organised programmes offering biennial mammography to women aged 50–69, with quality assurance, defined follow-up pathways and monitoring of performance.\n\n**Limited-resource settings with relatively strong health systems**: Mammography screening is not recommended as the first priority. The WHO suggests investing first in early diagnosis of symptomatic disease and in treatment capacity, and considering screening only once those are in place.\n\n**Limited-resource settings with weak health systems**: Focus on breast cancer awareness and timely diagnosis and treatment of symptomatic women. Clinical breast examination as a screening method should be offered only within research, because its effect on mortality has not been established.", "index_text": "WHO Breast Cancer Screening and Early Diagnosis > Recommendations by Resource Setting: **Well-resourced settings**: Organised programmes offering biennial mammography to women aged 50–69, with quality assurance, defined follow-up pathways and monitoring of performance.\n\n**Limited-resource settings with relatively strong health systems**: Mammography screening is not recommended as the first priority. The WHO suggests investing first in early diagnosis of symptomatic disease and in treatment capacity, and considering screening only once those are in place.\n\n**Limited-resource settings with weak health systems**: Focus on breast cancer awareness and timely diagnosis and treatment of symptomatic women. Clinical breast examination as a screening method should be offered only within research, because its effect on mortality has not been established."}
+{"chunk_id": "who_screening_protocols::global-breast-cancer-initiative::0", "source": "who_screening_protocols", "title": "WHO Breast Cancer Screening and Early Diagnosis", "section": "Global Breast Cancer Initiative", "text": "The WHO Global Breast Cancer Initiative (2021) aims to reduce breast cancer mortality by 2.5% per year, averting 2.5 million deaths between 2020 and 2040. It sets three targets: at least 60% of invasive breast cancers diagnosed at stage I or II (health promotion for early detection); diagnostic evaluation completed within 60 days of first presentation (timely diagnosis); and at least 80% of patients completing multimodality treatment (comprehensive management).", "index_text": "WHO Breast Cancer Screening and Early Diagnosis > Global Breast Cancer Initiative: The WHO Global Breast Cancer Initiative (2021) aims to reduce breast cancer mortality by 2.5% per year, averting 2.5 million deaths between 2020 and 2040. It sets three targets: at least 60% of invasive breast cancers diagnosed at stage I or II (health promotion for early detection); diagnostic evaluation completed within 60 days of first presentation (timely diagnosis); and at least 80% of patients completing multimodality treatment (comprehensive management)."}
+{"chunk_id": "who_screening_protocols::screening-effectiveness-data::0", "source": "who_screening_protocols", "title": "WHO Breast Cancer Screening and Early Diagnosis", "section": "Screening Effectiveness Data", "text": "In the randomised trials, inviting women aged 50–69 to mammographic screening reduced breast cancer mortality by about 20%; observational studies of women who actually attend suggest a larger reduction, around 40%. For women aged 40–49 the reduction in the trials was smaller, around 15%, with more false positives per cancer found. Screening has not been shown to reduce deaths from all causes.", "index_text": "WHO Breast Cancer Screening and Early Diagnosis > Screening Effectiveness Data: In the randomised trials, inviting women aged 50–69 to mammographic screening reduced breast cancer mortality by about 20%; observational studies of women who actually attend suggest a larger reduction, around 40%. For women aged 40–49 the reduction in the trials was smaller, around 15%, with more false positives per cancer found. Screening has not been shown to reduce deaths from all causes."}
